b 26551 Message bytecode: everything the game prints
D 26551 From the end of the dictionary (#R$67B7) up to the verb patterns at #R$8259, this is text in the message interpreter's bytecode (#R$A59F): a byte with bit 7 set starts a two-byte dictionary reference, $00-$1C are control codes (jump table at #R$A55D), $20-$3F are literal characters, $40-$5F introduce a run of packed 5-bit letters, and $60 and above are handled at #R$A83A.
D 26551 CORRECTED: the automatic code/data split had 23 'code' blocks in here, mostly starting on page boundaries (#R$6CFD($6D00), #R$6FFA($7000), ...). None of them is called or jumped to from anywhere; they came from this project's coverage map, which counted the printer's data reads as executed bytes. Two of them collided with message starts and broke the round trip, which is how this was found.
D 26551 212 messages are split out below: those printed by an LD HL shortly before a printer call, those loaded into HL or DE anywhere in code, those printed or said by plan scripts (PRINT and SAY TO, see #R$CC78), those only a room or thing record points to (room descriptions, for instance), and the rest, found by walking the bytecode from #R$67FF message by message. The 72 bytes before #R$67FF (this block) are not an ordinary message.
b 26615 Message: "YOU SAY"
D 26615 Rendered by running the game's own message interpreter (#R$A59F) in the emulator: "YOU SAY"
D 26615 Placeholders: the actor is "you", the direct object "the oil lamp", and ‹word› marks a word of the player's own command (control code $01), which needs parser state that no longer exists when rendering.
D 26615 No instruction loads it. It is called as a sub-message (control code $0B) at #R$67FF, #R$6804.
