b 38256 The node heap: 752 bytes for the engine's linked lists
D 38256 Free memory for #R$A11C (allocate) and #R$A1C7 (free). At start it is one free block: $02F0 bytes (byte 0-1), no next free block (2-3), pointed to by the free-list head at #R$9FE3. Every list node the engine makes lives here: sub-script call frames (#R$CF80), orders and remarks (#R$CDBF, #R$B8D1), SIGNAL tokens (#R$CD24) and countdowns (#R$E52F). A node of size n takes n + 3 bytes: a size byte and a 2-byte link sit in front of the address the allocator returns.
D 38256 CORRECTED: the automatic split had this as a 945-byte code block (the largest unannotated one), together with the actor table.
b 39008 The actor table: who acts, and where each is in its plan script
D 39008 24 eight-byte records, ended by $FF, walked once per turn. +0 the actor's thing id; +1/+2 its position in its plan script (see #R$CC78); +3 the plan's verb, +4/+5 the plan's direct and indirect objects ($FF = none; loaded by #R$D008, saved by #R$D026); +6/+7 the minute (counted like #R$9FDD from the start) at which the actor next acts, set by the main loop (#R$A064); 0 at the start, so everyone acts in the first minute. #R$CC0E finds a record by id. The seven steam trains and two cabbies are actors too.
D 39008 CORRECTED: earlier rounds read +1/+2 as a handler address; it is a script position, and it changes as the actor's script runs.
