b 39201 Plan script: you (actor 0) - read, parse and do the player's commands
D 39201 The player is a character like any other, and this is its script. Each pass: #R$D6BF reads a line from the keyboard with a time limit (#R$D898; if the player types nothing for a few seconds the step fails and the player WAITs instead, so the game goes on), #R$D715 turns the line (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00)) into a list of words at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B81)($5B81) (#R$D9C5), #R$D769 checks for words left, #R$D77E looks the next words up (#R$AAB2) and splits off one sentence (#R$9FF0 -> #R$9FF2), #R$D7D9 checks that there is a sentence, #R$D7FA parses it (#R$B191) into a verb and objects and copies them into the plan registers (#R$D826, #R$D82C), and DO plan verb / plan dobj / plan iobj carries it out. When a step fails the script goes back a step or two, which is how a line with several commands is worked through one sentence per turn. Read from the code; the five RUN targets are the same five addresses an earlier live trace saw at the start of every command.
D 39201 Confirmed in the emulator: 'TAKE LAMP. EXAMINE LAMP. INVENTORY' typed as one line is carried out one sentence a minute - the lamp is taken at 8:02, examined at 8:03 and the inventory listed at 8:04, with the clock printed between, before the next prompt.
D 39201 Entry points: #R$9921 = you (actor 0).
D 39201 Decoded by tools/plan.py; see #R$CC78 for the language. Each sub-block is one instruction. Room and object numbers are as the game uses them (the reference pages list both). Labels for verbs 128-140 and for the common helper records (#R$E52F, #R$E57A, #R$E588, #R$E5A8, #R$DDBE, #R$F198) were read from those records' code, not tried one by one in the emulator.
D 39201 In an instruction, 'plan dobj' and 'plan iobj' are not fixed objects: they mean the direct and indirect object currently held in the actor's plan registers (bytes +4 and +5 of its actor record), so the same step works on whatever the actor is dealing with - for the player, the objects of the command just typed. 'plan verb' is the verb register (+3), 'self' is the acting character, and '-' means none.
B 39201,2 DO 34 #R$DC7B(LOOK)
B 39203,5 RUN #R$D6BF [fail #R$9948]
B 39208,5 RUN #R$D715 [fail #R$9923]
B 39213,5 RUN #R$D769 [fail #R$9923]
B 39218,5 RUN #R$D77E [fail #R$9923]
B 39223,5 RUN #R$D7D9 [fail #R$992D]
B 39228,5 RUN #R$D7FA [fail #R$9937]
B 39233,4 DO plan verb: plan dobj, plan iobj
B 39237,3 GOTO #R$9937
B 39240,2 DO 49 #R$DDB4(WAIT HERE)
B 39242,3 GOTO #R$9923
b 39245 The case's relation words
D 39245 Word references for the verbs a statement about the case can use; a statement's relation number is a position here (1-based). #R$BDD1 finds the verb of what the player said in this list; #R$BF45 looks a relation up to print it (#R$BF1C prints a statement, with AND, word $005D, for relation 0).
