W 39247,2 Relation 2: HAD
W 39249,2 Relation 3: HAS
W 39251,2 Relation 4: IS
W 39253,2 Relation 5: KILLED
W 39255,2 Relation 6: VISITED
W 39257,2 Relation 7: HATED
W 39259,2 Relation 8: EXAMINED
W 39261,2 Relation 9: HID
W 39263,2 Relation 10: OWNS
W 39265,2 Relation 11: MADE
W 39267,2 End marker
b 39269 The case: statements about the crime, and the evidence each needs
D 39269 Eleven groups. Each starts with a statement record: +0 a thing or person, +1 a relation number, +2 another thing; +3 flags; and, with bit 4 or 5 of the flags, +4/+5 a routine run when it is proven. Bit 2 of the flags opens a group of evidence records (+0 and +2 are things, +1 is 0) and bit 3 closes it; #R$BE5A steps over one record and #R$BE72 over a whole group. In the state bits, bit 0 means the statement is waiting to be proven and bit 1 that it has been (#R$BCC9 sets bit 1 and runs the routine once the evidence checks out through #R$BF08; #R$9A74 does the same from a routine).
D 39269 With the relation words filled in (#R$994D) the statements read as sentences: MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES IS INNOCENT, MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF, MRS JONES KILLED TRICIA FENDER, PHIPPS HAS PLANS, SALE LOCATION IS OLD MILL ROAD, TRICIA FENDER HAD PLANS, TRICIA FENDER BLACKMAILED MRS BROWN, and four with no evidence and no routine (flags $B4: bit 5 set) saying that Tricia Fender or Phipps killed one of the two victims. What bit 5 does is not fully traced: #R$BCC9 only marks such a statement as waiting, and Watson's reply (#R$BA8C) answers it differently. The order is as stored (first thing, verb, second thing), which is also the order the player's words are matched in (#R$BD69). Some groups also hold a statement as evidence (MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES VISITED OPIUM DEN, under the Major's innocence). An evidence record with relation 0 is a pair of things that the player must have seen (#R$BE91: flags $00, both things' +6 bit 7) or examined (flags $40, +6 bit 6). Confirmed in the emulator that +6 bit 7 is set on everything in view in the first room and that EXAMINE LAMP sets bit 6 of the lamp's +6 ($80 -> $C0); the proving was tried too: Confirmed in the emulator (tools/experiments/case_test.py): with the player moved to Inspector Lestrade's room at the start, SAY TO LESTRADE "MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF" gets 'I do not believe that you have the evidence to prove that Mrs Brown killed herself' and the statement's flags go $A4 -> $A5 (bit 0: waiting). With the evidence poked in first - the female corpse and the sandstone bridge marked seen and examined (+6 bits 7 and 6), the large rock marked seen - the same words get 'So Mrs Brown killed herself, excellent Holmes, another brilliant conclusion.' and the flags go $A4 -> $A6 (bit 1: proven). Which order (subject first) the statements are matched in is read from #R$BD69.
D 39269 Also tried in the emulator: TRICIA FENDER KILLED MRS JONES (one of the bit-5 statements) told to Lestrade gets the same 'I do not believe that you have the evidence to prove that Tricia Fender killed Mrs Jones' and its flags go $B4 -> $B5; MRS JONES KILLED TRICIA FENDER gets the same answer with its flags $A4 -> $A5. Told to Watson, all three statements tried get 'That is brilliant Holmes...'. So nothing visible yet separates the bit-5 statements.
D 39269 How a piece of evidence is checked (#R$BE91, read from the code): the top two bits of its flags choose the test. $00: both things seen (+6 bit 7); $40: both examined (+6 bit 6); $80: never true by itself - it only counts once something else has marked it done (bit 1), as the statement routines do through #R$9A74, so a nested statement such as MAJOR PERCIVAL FFOULKES VISITED OPIUM DEN ($8C) has to be established first; $C0: no test of its own, only its group. A record with children (bit 2) also needs all of them (#R$BF08). Once a piece of evidence passes it is marked done and stays so.
D 39269 Confirmed in the emulator (tools/experiments/major_test.py): with the player moved to Lestrade at the start, MAJOR FFOULKES IS INNOCENT is marked waiting ($A4 -> $A5) and Lestrade at once asks 'Well can you prove that Major Percival Ffoulkes is innocent yet, Holmes ?'; MAJOR FFOULKES VISITED THE OPIUM DEN gets 'I do not believe that you have the evidence...'. With bit 1 of #R$996F($9972) set first - as #R$F103 does when the Major confesses - the opium-den statement is proven, the waiting innocence statement is proven with it (#R$9965($9968) $A5 -> $A6), #R$9A3D sets #R$9E87($9E88) and Lestrade says 'Very good Holmes, I shall return to Leather head tomorrow to have another look around.'
D 39269 This is what Lestrade's 'WELL CAN YOU PROVE THAT ... YET, HOLMES ?' (#R$9DD8, through #R$BC46-#R$BC9C) is about, and the routines here are the 'handlers' at #R$99FC-#R$9A7C (overlay 07).
