w 40875 Verbs 128-140: the verbs only plan scripts use
D 40875 A DO instruction in a plan script with a verb number of 128 or more comes here instead of to the parser's verb table: #R$C4D1 sends verbs with bit 7 set to #R$C583, which takes the record for verb 128+n from entry n. The player can never type these. Records with flags bit 7 set are sub-scripts.
D 40875 Confirmed in the emulator over 84 turns: verb #R$E5AD(131) succeeded 864 times, every time with the direct object really in the room named by the indirect object; verb #R$E497(128) ran 4 times (Inspector Lestrade moving himself to room 53, the Major to room 92, and two others) and each time the object ended up in the named room. Verb #R$F1D6(136) is checked in the Trivia entry on appointments. The other verbs' labels are read from their records' code.
D 40875 Verb #R$E5D8(129) was checked the same way: in 84 turns its comparison ran 482 times and came out equal exactly when the two things' rooms (record +$0F) were equal.
D 40875 CORRECTED: an earlier version said where the index came from was not traced and that some records 'do not read as code' - they are plan scripts.
D 40875 Found the hard way: once the verb default table at #R$9F38 was marked as data, the automatic split's code block starting at #R$9FC3 - in the middle of this table - collided with the action record at #R$9FC5, and the round trip failed until this table was marked as data too.
W 40875,2 Verb #R$E497(128): #R$E497 - PUT: move the direct object into room (indirect object); room 0 is off stage
W 40877,2 Verb #R$E5D8(129): #R$E5D8 - IS WITH: are the direct and indirect objects in the same room?
W 40879,2 Verb #R$E5FD(130): #R$E5FD - IS HERE: is the direct object with the actor (#R$C863)?
W 40881,2 Verb #R$E5AD(131): #R$E5AD - IS IN: is the direct object in room (indirect object)?
W 40883,2 Verb #R$F210(132): #R$F210 - LOCK: set bit 0 (locked) of the direct object's +5 flags
W 40885,2 Verb #R$F203(133): #R$F203 - UNLOCK: clear bit 0 (locked) of the direct object's +5 flags
W 40887,2 Verb #R$EB8B(134): #R$EB8B - sub-script: FOLLOW the direct object until in room (indirect object)
W 40889,2 Verb #R$ED28(135): #R$ED28 - sub-script: OPEN, GO THROUGH and CLOSE a door
W 40891,2 Verb #R$F1D6(136): #R$F1D6 - IS HOUR: is the clock's hour (1-24) the direct object?
W 40893,2 Verb #R$EB9B(137): #R$EB9B - sub-script: do verb (direct object), then EXAMINE the indirect object
W 40895,2 Verb #R$EDE9(138): #R$EDE9 - sub-script: go through a door (135), then CHANCE (indirect object)
W 40897,2 Verb #R$EDDD(139): #R$EDDD - sub-script: do verb (direct object), then CHANCE (indirect object)
W 40899,2 Verb #R$F2A5(140): #R$F2A5 - sub-script: #R$E4C9 on the direct object, then WAIT (indirect object) minutes
c 40901 Default action: KILL (also BREAK and a profane synonym)
D 40901 The pre-check is the body itself: it always refuses, handing back #R$9FCE, which prints message #R$680B. Confirmed in the emulator: BREAK LAMP and KILL WATSON both answer 'ONLY AN IDIOT WOULD TRY THAT.'
R 40901 Output:HL $9FCE, the routine that prints the refusal
R 40901 A 0 (refused)
B 40901,4 Action record header: flags $40; bit 6: pre-check at #R$9FC9; byte 1 $00
C 40905 Always refuse...
C 40908 Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
C 40909 Return to the caller
C 40910 "ONLY AN IDIOT WOULD TRY THAT."
C 40913 Jump on to the routine "Print the message at HL" (#R$A59F)
s 40916
b 40918 Game variables
D 40918 The plan-script registers, the clock and the heads of the engine's linked lists (each list's head word doubles as the link of a pseudo-node 2 bytes further on, which is the address the list walk #R$A0F7 starts from: #R$9FE7 for countdowns, #R$9FE9 for tokens, #R$9FEB for frames, #R$9FF0 for orders). The clock fields were read from #R$BFD9; the rest from their users. Bytes not listed are not identified yet: among them #R$A002($A009) (the room the player is in, which decides what is shown), #R$A00A (the current actor's room), #R$A00A($A00F)/#R$A00A($A010) (requirement codes), #R$A012($A013)/#R$A012($A015) (the direct and indirect objects' records), #R$A01A($A01F) (minutes the action takes), #R$A032($A033)-#R$A032($A036) (verb, direct object, indirect object, actor).
D 40918 CORRECTED: the automatic split had this as code.
B 40918,1 #R$9FD6: the plan's verb (argument $FA)
B 40919,1 #R$9FD7: the plan's direct object (argument $FD)
B 40920,1 #R$9FD8: the plan's indirect object (argument $FC)
W 40921,2 #R$9FD9: the plan instruction being run
W 40923,2 #R$9FDB: the current actor's record in the actor table
W 40925,2 #R$9FDD: minutes since the start
B 40927,1 #R$9FDF: the minute
B 40928,1 #R$9FE0: the hour, 1-12
B 40929,1 #R$9FE1: the day (0 = Monday)
B 40930,1 #R$9FE2: 'a' or 'p'
W 40931,2 #R$9FE3: the heap's free list
W 40933,2 #R$9FE5: countdowns (2-byte nodes: actor, count)
W 40935,2 #R$9FE7: SIGNAL tokens (2-byte nodes: from, to)
W 40937,2 #R$9FE9: sub-script call frames (7-byte nodes)
B 40939,1 #R$9FEB: not identified (#R$D781 clears it)
W 40940,2 #R$9FEC: quoted speech waiting to be handed over (#R$B049, #R$B86A)
W 40942,2 #R$9FEE: orders and remarks waiting for actors (8-byte nodes)
W 40944,2 #R$9FF0: words the player has said, for the next remark
c 41024 Start the game
D 41024 The game's own entry point (the tape loader jumps here once everything is loaded - see loader_notes.md). Clears two flag bytes, resets the stack to #R$5D80 (reusing the loader's own workspace, which is no longer needed), and clears the screen.
D 41024 Then it waits for a single keypress to choose New Game vs Continue: pressing 'n' or 'N' starts a new game (clears a flag at #R$A00A($A011)); anything else leaves that flag set, and the code that follows loads state back from a table at #R$9860, so this is very likely also how a restored/continued game resumes its characters' positions (not yet confirmed with an actual saved game in hand - see verification.md's 'say which things are verified' guidance).
D 41024 Seeds a simple RNG from the Z80 refresh register (R) - a common period-accurate trick, since R free-runs on every instruction fetch and so is effectively unpredictable at the moment the player first presses a key.
D 41024 CORRECTED 2026-11-07: the actor-table walk that follows is not part of start-up. From #R$A064 on is the game's main loop - see there.
R 41024 Output:F None: this routine never returns, it falls into the main loop
C 41024 Clear the 'currently examining' flag
C 41025 Store A in "A flag of the player's script step, and spare..." (#R$D6B8)
C 41028 Clear another one-shot flag
C 41031 No interrupts while we set things up
C 41032 Clear the screen and set up the text window
C 41035 Stack now safely below the game's own code/data
C 41038 Wait here for the player's very first keypress
C 41041 Did they press lowercase 'n'?
C 41043 If A equals $6E, skip ahead to #R$A05B
C 41045 ...or uppercase 'N'?
C 41047 Default: treat as "continue" (flag = 1)
C 41049 If A differs from $4E, skip ahead to #R$A05C
C 41051 "New game" was chosen instead (flag = 0)
C 41052 Store A in #R$A00A($A011) in "Game variables"
C 41055 A = the free-running refresh register...
C 41057 ...used as an unpredictable RNG seed
c 41060 The main loop: one minute of game time per pass
D 41060 Each pass is one minute. The clock moves on (#R$BFD9) and is printed (#R$BF79); then every actor in the table at #R$9860 whose next-turn time (+6/+7) has come takes its turn. A turn starts with #R$CC37 (find the actor, check for orders or remarks waiting for it) and then runs plan-script steps (#R$CC78) until one takes time (#R$A01A($A01F) non-zero); that many minutes are added to the actor's next-turn time, and the actor's own after-turn routine, if it has one, runs (#R$9C77). The player is an actor like the rest: the player's script step is the one that waits for a typed command.
D 41060 Before the walk, if the player is in the middle of WAIT UNTIL (plan verb #R$DDD0(50)) and ENTER is pressed, the player's next-turn time is pulled back to now, so a long wait can be cut short.
D 41060 #R$A0D9($A0DA) is set at the end of the minute's walk to leave the loop: 1 (#R$B0AC, after a game-over message) loads a saved game (#R$B0D7) and starts again from #R$A040; 2 (#R$B0C6, not traced back to the command that sets it) saves the game (#R$B0E4) and carries on.
D 41060 Confirmed in the emulator: the player's +6/+7 equals the minute counter (#R$9FDD) whenever the game waits for a command, and moves on by 5 for WAIT and by 1 for TAKE LAMP or EXAMINE LAMP; Watson's and Lestrade's move on in step. The per-actor call counts in the #R$B91F note (#R$CC37 once per actor per turn) agree.
D 41060 How it works:
D 41060 1. Clear the ending flag #R$A0D9($A0DA). If it is set on a later pass, go to the ending code at #R$A0E7.
D 41060 2. Move the clock on one minute (#R$BFD9) and print it (#R$BF79).
D 41060 3. If the player is waiting until a time (plan verb #R$DDD0(50)) and ENTER is held down, make the player's next turn now.
D 41060 4. Walk the actor table from #R$9860 in 8-byte steps until the $FF marker. For each actor whose time has come (#R$A0DB): start its turn (#R$CC37), then run plan steps (#R$CC78) until one takes time (#R$A01A($A01F) non-zero).
D 41060 5. Add that time to the actor's next-turn time and run the actor's after-turn routine (#R$9C77).
D 41060 6. At the end of the table, loop for the next minute. When the ending flag is 2 the game is saved (#R$B0E4) and the loop continues; otherwise it is loaded (#R$B0D7) and the game restarts at #R$A040.
R 41060 Output:F None: the loop never ends by itself; $A0DA stops it
C 41060 Clear A to zero (and the carry flag) - Not ending yet
C 41061 Store A in #R$A0D9($A0DA) in "The main loop: one minute of game time per..."
C 41064 Read #R$A0D9($A0DA) in "The main loop: one minute of game time per..." into A - Is the game ending?
C 41067 Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears carry)
C 41068 Yes: restart or reload
C 41070 A minute passes
C 41073 Print the clock
C 41076 The player (first actor)...
C 41080 Read byte +3 of the record at IY into A - ...waiting until a time (plan verb #R$DDD0(50))?
C 41083 Compare A with $32
C 41085 If A differs from $32, skip ahead to #R$A093
