C 41193 If A equals $02, skip ahead to #R$A0F1
C 41195 Load: restore the saved game (#R$B0D7)...
C 41198 ...and carry on at #R$A040
C 41201 Save: write the game out (#R$B0E4)...
C 41204 ...and carry on at #R$A064
c 41207 Move IX to the next node of a list
D 41207 Every engine list links its nodes through the 2 bytes in front of each node (IX-2/IX-1). Z at the end of the list.
R 41207 Input:IX A node of a list
R 41207 Output:IX The next node
R 41207 F Z at the end of the list (A = 0)
C 41207 Save HL on the stack - HL = IX's link (IX-2), the next node; A = 0 and Z at the end of the list
C 41208 Read byte -2 of the record at IX into L
C 41211 Read byte -1 of the record at IX into H
C 41214 Copy L into A
C 41215 Set in A the bits that are set in H
C 41216 Swap HL with the word on top of the stack - IX = the next node
C 41217 Restore IX from the stack
C 41219 Return to the caller
c 41220 Find the node before node DE in the list at IY
D 41220 Leaves IY at the node (or list head) whose link points at DE, so the caller can unlink DE.
R 41220 Input:IY The list head
R 41220 DE The node to find
R 41220 Output:IY The node (or list head) whose link points at DE
C 41220 Step IY on to the next byte - Start after the list head's own link
C 41222 Step IY on to the next byte
C 41224 Read byte -2 of the record at IY into L - HL = the link (the next node's address) at IY - 2
C 41227 Read byte -1 of the record at IY into H
C 41230 Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears carry)
C 41231 Save HL on the stack
C 41232 Subtract DE and the carry from HL - Is it node DE? Then IY is the node before it
C 41234 If the two values are equal, skip ahead to #R$A118
C 41236 Restore IY from the stack - No: step to that node and look again
C 41238 Go back to #R$A108
C 41240 Restore HL from the stack
C 41241 Return to the caller
b 41242 $A11C: the free-list link before the best block so far
D 41242 Addresses named in the title: #R$A11C.
W 41242,2 Link address
c 41244 Allocate A + 3 bytes from the free list, cleared
D 41244 Walks the free blocks from #R$9FE3 (each starts with its size and a link) and takes the smallest that is big enough - best fit. If what would be left is under 4 bytes the whole block is unlinked, otherwise it is split. The block is zeroed, its size stored before it, and IX returned on it with NZ. No block big enough: #R$C1D8 (out of memory). Word lists, remarks and orders are built in these blocks; #R$A1AF frees them. Read from the code.
D 41244 Checked 9 November over the first 120 commands of the winning route: 2957 runs, every one met its stated postcondition (the block from IX-2 for A+2 bytes is zero, IX is non-zero, and the size byte at IX-3 is A+3).
D 41244 How it works:
D 41244 1. C = the number of bytes wanted + 3 (the size field and the link).
D 41244 2. Walk the free list from #R$9FE3. Each free block begins with its size and a link. Blocks that are too small are passed over.
D 41244 3. Of those that are big enough, remember the smallest (best fit), and the link that points to it.
D 41244 4. If none fits, go to the out-of-memory routine at #R$C1D8.
D 41244 5. If the chosen block would leave 4 bytes or more, split it: the free block keeps the front part and the end is handed out. Otherwise the whole block is taken and unhooked from the free list.
D 41244 6. Store the size before the block, clear its data and return NZ with IX at the block.
R 41244 Input:A The number of data bytes wanted (3 more are used for the header)
R 41244 Output:IX The cleared block
R 41244 F NZ on success; out of memory ends in $C1D8
C 41244 Save IY on the stack - Save IY, HL, DE, BC
C 41246 Save HL on the stack
C 41247 Save DE on the stack
C 41248 Save BC on the stack
C 41249 Add $03 to A - C = the size wanted, A + 3
C 41251 Copy A into C
C 41252 B bit 0: a block has been chosen
C 41254 Read the heap's free list (#R$9FE3) into HL - HL = the first free block; IY = the link that points to it
C 41257 Point IY at the heap's free list (#R$9FE3)
C 41261 Copy L into A - End of the free list?
C 41262 Set in A the bits that are set in H
C 41263 If the result is zero, jump on to #R$A160
C 41265 Read the byte HL points at into E - DE = this block's size; HL on to its link
C 41266 Step HL on to the next byte
C 41267 Read the byte HL points at into D
C 41268 Step HL on to the next byte
C 41269 Copy D into A - A size over 255 is always big enough
C 41270 Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears carry)
C 41271 If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$A13D
C 41273 Copy E into A - Under 256: too small for C?
C 41274 Compare A with C
C 41275 Yes: next block
C 41277 Test bit 0 of B - Big enough. Nothing chosen yet?
C 41279 If bit 0 of B is clear, skip ahead to #R$A14E
C 41281 Save HL on the stack - Otherwise is it smaller than the chosen block (size at IX-2)?
C 41282 Read byte -2 of the record at IX into L
C 41285 Read byte -1 of the record at IX into H
C 41288 Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction that follows is a plain one (a compare)
C 41289 Subtract DE from HL (a compare: carry = HL was smaller, zero = equal)
C 41291 Restore HL from the stack
C 41292 No: keep the one we have
C 41294 Save HL on the stack - Choose it: IX = its link field
C 41295 Restore IX from the stack
C 41297 Store IY in "#R$A11C: the free-list link before the best block..." (#R$A11A) - and remember the link that points to it
C 41301 Set bit 0 of B
C 41303 Save HL on the stack - IY = this block's link field; follow it to the next block
C 41304 Restore IY from the stack
C 41306 Read the byte HL points at into E
C 41307 Step HL on to the next byte
C 41308 Read the byte HL points at into D
C 41309 Swap DE and HL
C 41310 Go back to #R$A12D
