c 41700 Narrate a command as a sentence ('You take the oil lamp.')
D 41700 Saves the verb and objects (#R$A35D-#R$A35F), then - unless it is too dark and the player is the one acting, when it prints 'SORRY IT IS TOO DARK.' (#R$68D1) - prints the actor (#R$A724), the verb's words from its pattern (#R$A2B1/#R$A2BD) and the objects, ending the line (#R$A2A2). This is the sentence every successful action shows, for the player and for characters in sight ('Inspector Lestrade hails the hansom cab.'). Read from the code; the output it describes is what the emulator shows.
D 41700 .
D 41700 Line comments added from reading the code. What #R$A00A($A00C) means (the B of this routine) was not established, so the two narration paths are described by the branch, not by who is acting.
D 41700 How it works:
D 41700 1. Set #R$A2AA to 1 while narrating, and keep the verb and both objects at #R$A35D to #R$A35F.
D 41700 2. Work out whether the text is printed as quoted (#R$A022($A027) is 1 when #R$A00A($A00C) is 0).
D 41700 3. If it is too dark to see (#R$D078) and the actor is the player, print 'SORRY IT IS TOO DARK.' and finish.
D 41700 4. Find the verb's record and gather its flags (#R$A2BD). If bit 4 of #R$A01A($A01D) is set the verb is never narrated.
D 41700 5. Start a fresh line if the actor is a character other than the player, Watson or Lestrade's usual set (#R$DC6C), and print the actor's name (#R$A724).
D 41700 6. If the actor was the player print the word YOU and narrate the command (#R$A360); otherwise narrate it as it stands (#R$A37D). End the line, clear #R$A2AA and #R$A022($A027).
R 41700 Input:$A033 The verb
R 41700 $A034 The first object, $A035 the second
R 41700 $A036 The actor
R 41700 Output:All registers kept
