c 42189 Do the pattern words at IY name the words at HL? ($A4CD, $A4E0)
D 42189 Addresses named in the title: #R$A4E0.
D 42189 #R$A4E0: the first pair must match (#R$A51B; an empty pattern word matches anything), then the next two pairs are tried both ways round, so two adjectives can be typed in either order; A = 0 for a match in the order given and 1 for a match with the second and third pairs swapped (the verb lookup's #R$B405 then swaps #R$B185($B188) and #R$B185($B18A) to suit); #R$A02A($A02E) is set to 1 as soon as the first pair matches. NZ means no match. #R$A4CD first requires the fourth pair (six bytes on) to match, and is used by the verb lookup (#R$B3EB($B3EC)); #R$A4E0 by the thing and room searches (#R$D37A($D37B), #R$D3B7($D3B8)) and the scenery check (#R$B763($B764)). Read from the code.
D 42189 How it works:
D 42189 1. A4CD tries the fourth pair of the pattern first, six bytes on; if it fails, the whole match fails.
D 42189 2. A4E0 then needs the first pair to match (#R$A51B; an empty pattern word matches anything) and notes that in #R$A02A($A02E).
D 42189 3. The second and third pairs are tried as they stand (A = 0).
D 42189 4. If that fails they are tried swapped, the pattern's third against the command's second and its second against their third (A = 1).
R 42189 Input:HL The words of a command (four pairs)
R 42189 IY The pattern words
R 42189 Output:F Z when the pattern names the words
R 42189 A 0 when the second and third pairs matched as they stand; 1 when they matched swapped
R 42189 Registers HL, IY, DE kept
C 42189 Save DE on the stack
