C 42276 Flip in A the bits that are set in the byte HL points at
C 42277 Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F)
C 42279 If any of bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A was set, skip ahead to #R$A52E
C 42281 Step HL back one byte - ...and the low bytes: a 12-bit match
C 42282 Read the byte HL points at into A
C 42283 Compare A with the first byte of the record at IY
C 42286 Restore HL from the stack - Either way both pointers move on two bytes
C 42287 Step HL on to the next byte
C 42288 Step HL on to the next byte
C 42289 Step IY on to the next byte
C 42291 Step IY on to the next byte
C 42293 Return to the caller
c 42294 Wait for a key, but not for ever
D 42294 Polls the keyboard (#R$C41F) up to #R$A03A($A03B) times (reset to 3,000 each call), with a 256-step delay loop between polls. A key: returns it with bit 7 cleared. No key in that time: rubs out whatever has been typed on the line (#R$D9B7) and returns 0 with Z set. Read from the code; the real-time length of the limit was not measured (the harness answers key reads itself), but the give-up path was seen working - see #R$D898.
R 42294 Output:A The key pressed (0 if none)
R 42294 F Z when nothing was typed in time
C 42294 Save HL on the stack
C 42295 How many polls before giving up
C 42298 A key?
C 42301 Test bit 7 of A - Yes
C 42303 If bit 7 of A is set, skip ahead to #R$A553
C 42305 Load A with $00 - No: a short delay...
C 42307 Subtract one from A
C 42308 Save HL on the stack
C 42309 Restore HL from the stack
C 42310 If the zero flag is clear, go back to #R$A543
C 42312 Step HL back one byte - ...and count a poll
C 42313 Copy H into A
C 42314 Set in A the bits that are set in L
C 42315 If the result is not zero, go back to #R$A53A - Try again
C 42317 Restore HL from the stack
C 42318 Save HL on the stack
C 42319 Time is up: rub out the line
C 42322 Z: nothing typed
C 42323 The key
C 42325 Load HL with $0BB8 - Reset the limit for next time
C 42328 Store HL in #R$A03A($A03B) in "Game variables"
C 42331 Restore HL from the stack
C 42332 Return to the caller
w 42333 Routines for the message control codes $00-$1F
D 42333 One address per control code, used by #R$A621. Codes below $1D are called and the interpreter carries on; $1D-$1F are jumped to instead (#R$A62F). CORRECTED: the automatic split had all but the first entry as code.
D 42333 What the handlers do, from reading them (2026-11-08; codes not listed were not worked out): $00 print the thing whose address is the next parameter; $01 print the next parameter as a word; $05 print the case statement at the next parameter; $06 name the current actor; $07 name the direct object; $08 print the character in A; $09 name the indirect object; $0A pick one of the next n elements at random; $0B print a message at an offset from here; $0C YOUR for the player, HIS for anyone else (the actor); $0D print the character in A; $0E YOUR for the player, HIS for anyone else (the direct object); $0F only in part of the day 5; $10 name the actor with the verb form (ARE / IS); $11 name the direct object with the verb form; $13 name the person in the next parameter with the verb form; $14 only in part of the day 4; $15 only in part of the day 3; $16 only in part of the day 2; $17 only in part of the day 1; $18 narrate the remembered command; $19 print the message at the next parameter; $1D end the message, no full stop, with a newline; $1E end the message with a full stop and a newline; $1F does nothing.
