w 42397 Where the message's stack parameters end
D 42397 Set by #R$A5A9 to the stack pointer as it was before the caller pushed the message's parameters; #R$A5BF reloads it on the way out, so the printer itself throws the parameters away.
W 42397,2 Stack pointer to return with
c 42399 Print the message at HL
D 42399 The message interpreter's entry point; almost every word the game prints comes through here (see the message area at #R$67B7 for the bytecode). Parameters - extra messages or words, such as what a character says - are pushed on the stack before the call and control codes pick them up; on the way out the stack pointer is reset past them (#R$A59D), so callers push and never pop. Saves the registers, runs the byte loop (#R$A5C4 / #R$A5CD) and returns through JP (HL). Read from the code; the rendering of 118 messages in the emulator relies on it.
D 42399 How it works:
D 42399 1. Save the registers and take the address of the caller's parameters (stack pointer plus 12) for #R$A59D.
D 42399 2. Clear #R$A00A($A00C) unless #R$A00A($A00B) is set.
D 42399 3. Run the interpreter over the message (#R$A5C4).
D 42399 4. Restore the registers, drop the parameters from the stack and return.
R 42399 Input:HL The message
R 42399 (SP+) Any parameters the caller pushed, dropped on return
R 42399 Output:All registers kept
C 42399 Save BC on the stack - Print message HL: save the registers
C 42400 Save DE on the stack
C 42401 Save IX on the stack
C 42403 Save AF on the stack
C 42404 Save HL on the stack
C 42405 Load HL with $000C - HL = SP + 12: the caller's parameters
C 42408 Add SP to HL
C 42409 Save the message pointer in (#R$A59D)
C 42412 Restore HL
C 42413 Read #R$A00A($A00B) in "Game variables" into A - #R$A00A($A00B) set? Then skip the next store
C 42416 Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears carry)
C 42417 If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$A5B6
C 42419 Otherwise #R$A00A($A00C) = 0
C 42422 Call the routine "Interpret message bytes until the end of the message" (#R$A5C4) - Interpret the message
C 42425 Restore AF from the stack
C 42426 Restore IX from the stack
C 42428 Restore DE from the stack
C 42429 Restore BC from the stack
C 42430 The return address
C 42431 Drop the parameters
C 42435 Return
c 42436 Interpret message bytes until the end of the message
D 42436 IX walks the message; #R$A5CD handles one element - a dictionary word (bit 7 set: two bytes), packed letters, a literal character or a control code (#R$A621) - and returns Z at the end.
D 42436 Only the driver loop: #R$A5CD does the work for each element of the message (its own block, with the element types listed there). Every message the game prints goes through here.
D 42436 Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over the 15 stored routes): the body ran 77,495 times in 14 of them. Every one of its 5 instructions was executed (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
