c 42445 Interpret one message element at IX
D 42445 Bit 7 set: a two-byte dictionary reference, printed by #R$A887 - unless its flag nibble is $20, $30 or $60, which #R$A647 treats as references to the command's own words (actor, objects). $00-$1F: a control code (#R$A621). $20-$3F: that character. $40-$5F: a run of (low 5 bits + 1) letters packed 5 bits each, unpacked by #R$A990. $60 and up: #R$A83A. Read from the code; it is what the 118 rendered messages went through.
D 42445 How it works:
D 42445 1. Bit 7 set: a two-byte dictionary reference. Classes $20, $30 and $60 are the sentence-ending words (#R$A647); any other is printed (#R$A887).
D 42445 2. Codes $20 to $3F: a single literal character. $40 to $5F: a run of (code AND $1F) + 1 letters packed five bits each (#R$A990), each printed as it is unpacked.
D 42445 3. $60 and above: one of the common words (#R$A83A).
D 42445 4. Below $20: a control code, which is looked up in the handler table at #R$A55D and called. Codes below $1D carry on; $1D and above end the message.
R 42445 Input:IX The message position
R 42445 Output:F NZ to go on with the next element; Z when the message has ended
R 42445 IX Moved on
