c 43447 Print character A, a word at a time (word wrap)
D 43447 Every character the message interpreter prints comes here. Unless quoted printing is on (#R$A022($A027): straight to #R$C1E1), characters are collected in the buffer at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B), a backspace (8) taking the last one off again - which is how suffixes such as '<delete>IES' work. A space, a carriage return or a quote mark ends the word: if the word is longer than the count at #R$C1F5 allows, a new line is started first, and then the word is sent to #R$AA14 character by character. #R$AA14 turns letters to lower case and drops spaces at the start of a line (#R$A022($A025) = 0 there). Read from the code.
D 43447 How it works:
D 43447 1. If quoted printing is on (#R$A022($A027)), send the character straight out (#R$C1E1).
D 43447 2. A space, carriage return or quote ends a word. Any other character is added to the word buffer at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C0B)($5C0B); a delete removes the last letter.
D 43447 3. When a word ends: if it does not fit on the rest of the line (#R$C1F5), start a new line first. Then print the word, and then the character that ended it.
R 43447 Input:A The character to print
R 43447 Output:All registers kept
