c 43698 Parse the next sentence from the word list
D 43698 Called for the player's typed line (#R$D78E($D78F), part of the player's script step #R$D77E) and for the words of a remark (#R$B8BE($B8BF)). Sorts tokens into the parse record (IY) - the verb and its particles (#R$AB91), objects (#R$AC24) - and dispatches on punctuation: a full stop finishes the sentence (#R$AD57), a comma starts another object or sentence (#R$ACCC), a special word (class $90) goes to #R$AFB3. NZ if it does not make sense (#R$AF67). Read from the code; only its outline was traced.
D 43698 How it works:
D 43698 1. Clear the sentence count and start with every kind of word allowed (E = $FF).
D 43698 2. Decide the sentence's mode (#R$A022($A024)). If a line of sentences is still being worked through it is an ordinary command (mode 1). Otherwise try to read a noun phrase first (#R$AC24): a sentence that opens with one and has no preposition is a statement such as MRS JONES KILLED TRICIA FENDER (mode 2, subject kept in the parse node); anything else is a command (mode 1) and reading starts again from the beginning.
D 43698 3. Read the verb and its particles (#R$AB91). Carry means the words make no sense.
D 43698 4. A special word (token class $90) goes to #R$AFB3. A comma ($A0) starts another sentence (#R$ACCC).
D 43698 5. Read the objects: each noun phrase (#R$AC24) is filed in the parse node (#R$ACA6), and this repeats as long as noun phrases follow.
D 43698 6. A full stop ($B0) completes the sentence (#R$AD57). In a statement, any other word must be a relation word (#R$ABF3), which ends the subject and carries on with the object.
D 43698 7. Anything else makes no sense: return NZ (#R$AF67).
R 43698 Input:IY The parse node for the sentence
R 43698 Output:F NZ if the sentence makes no sense (A = 2 for a stray full stop)
