c 43921 Read a sentence's verb words into the node at IY
D 43921 Tokens (#R$AE7F) of class $30 (a verb, see #R$AE7F) go to IY+0/+1, class $00 to IY+2/+3, $40 and $20 to one or the other by the flags in E; $E0 tokens are skipped; anything else ends the verb (carry when a slot was wanted twice). In statement mode (#R$A022($A024) = 2, while a case statement is being said) the verb must instead be one of the eleven relation words at #R$994D - KILLED, HAD, ... - and NZ means it is not. Read from the code; classes $00, $20, $40 and $E0 were not seen in the emulator.
D 43921 .
D 43921 Line comments added from reading the code; the meaning of the E register bits is inferred from how they are tested.
D 43921 How it works:
D 43921 1. In statement mode (#R$A022($A024) = 2) the words are matched against the relation words at #R$994D instead (#R$ABF3).
D 43921 2. Otherwise take tokens (#R$AE7F). A class $30 token goes in the verb slot (IY+0/+1), a class $00 token in the second word slot (IY+2/+3).
D 43921 3. A class $40 token clears E bit 0 and is treated as a verb; a class $20 token goes to whichever slot is free.
D 43921 4. A word for a slot already taken returns carry set. Any other class ends the verb: return Z if the verb slot has been filled.
R 43921 Input:IY The sentence node
R 43921 E Slot flags (bit 0, 1, 2)
R 43921 Output:F Z if the verb slot was filled; NZ if not; carry set for a second word in a slot already taken
R 43921 E Updated
C 43921 Read #R$A022($A024) in "Game variables" into A - Statement mode (#R$A022($A024) = 2)?
C 43924 Compare A with $02
C 43926 Yes: the relation-word check at #R$ABF3
C 43928 Copy E into A - E bits 0-2 set: ready for a verb slot (bit 1), an object-word slot (bit 2), and the direct-or-indirect choice (bit 0)
C 43929 Set bits 0, 1, 2 of A (mask $07)
C 43931 Copy A into E
C 43932 Next token (#R$AE7F): class in A, word reference in BC
C 43935 Compare A with $E0 - Class $E0: skipped
C 43937 If A equals $E0, go back to #R$AB9C
C 43939 Compare A with $30 - Class $30 (verb word)?
C 43941 If A differs from $30, skip ahead to #R$ABB7
C 43943 Verb slot still free (E bit 1)?
C 43945 No: wanted twice, carry (#R$ABB5)
C 43947 Fill the verb slot: mark it taken...
C 43949 Store C in the first byte of the record at IY - ...and store the word reference at IY+0/+1
C 43952 Store B in byte +1 of the record at IY
C 43955 Go back to #R$AB9C
C 43957 Set the carry flag for the caller - Carry: a second word for a slot already taken
C 43958 Return to the caller
C 43959 Compare A with $00 - Class $00 (second word)?
C 43961 If A differs from $00, skip ahead to #R$ABC9
C 43963 Test bit 2 of E - Its slot (E bit 2) free?
C 43965 If bit 2 of E is clear, go back to #R$ABB5
C 43967 Clear bit 2 of E - Mark taken and store at IY+2/+3
C 43969 Store C in byte +2 of the record at IY
C 43972 Store B in byte +3 of the record at IY
C 43975 Go back to #R$AB9C
C 43977 Compare A with $40 - Class $40: clear E bit 0, then treat as a verb word
C 43979 If A differs from $40, skip ahead to #R$ABD1
C 43981 Clear bit 0 of E
C 43983 Go back to #R$ABA7
C 43985 Compare A with $20 - Class $20: goes to whichever slot is free:
C 43987 If A differs from $20, skip ahead to #R$ABEF
C 43989 Test bit 1 of E - verb slot free: use it
C 43991 If bit 1 of E is set, go back to #R$ABA7
C 43993 Test bit 0 of E - bit 0 still set: use the second slot
C 43995 If bit 0 of E is set, go back to #R$ABBB
C 43997 Clear bit 2 of E - else copy the verb word into the second slot and take the verb slot
C 43999 Set bit 0 of E
C 44001 Read the first byte of the record at IY into A
C 44004 Store A in byte +2 of the record at IY
C 44007 Read byte +1 of the record at IY into A
C 44010 Store A in byte +3 of the record at IY
C 44013 Go back to #R$ABAB
C 44015 Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears carry) - Any other class ends the verb: Z if the verb slot has been filled (E bit 1 clear), NZ if not
C 44016 Test bit 1 of E
C 44018 Return to the caller
