c 45084 Handlers for BUT, ALL and a quote mark
D 45084 #R$B01C (BUT, after ALL) and #R$B033 (ALL) set #R$A01A to 2 and 1 for 'all but' and 'all'. #R$B049 (a quote mark): the words that follow are speech; their position is added to the list at #R$9FEC and the parser skips to the closing quote, marking it as the end of a sentence. #R$B86A hands them on when SAY TO / TALK TO runs. #R$B09A (PAUSE) waits (#R$C415). #R$B0A3 (LOAD, and QUIT too) goes to #R$B0AC: the game asks for a saved-game tape. Read from the code.
D 45084 Confirmed in the emulator: QUIT and LOAD both print 'PLAY SAVED GAME TAPE TO BE LOADED' and set #R$A0D9($A0DA) to 1; PAUSE and NOPRINT are accepted with no message.
D 45084 Tried in the emulator 2026-11-08: a line that is only a quotation, "HELLO", is taken as TALK (the word at $088B): 'You talk to Watson.' and Watson's good morning, the same as SAY "HELLO". TO WATSON "HELLO" gets WHAT ?.
D 45084 Played: each quotation in a SAY TO / TALK TO is added to the list at #R$9FEC (#R$B049) whether or not the command later succeeds; #R$B86A hands on the first node. A SAY that failed (I SEE NO ... THAT YOU CAN TALK TO, no answer) therefore leaves a stale quotation in front of the next one, and the next person spoken to hears the old words. TALK TO <person> with no quotation empties the list (2 nodes to 0 in the emulator).
