C 45304 Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction that follows is a plain one (a compare)
C 45305 Subtract DE from HL (a compare: carry = HL was smaller, zero = equal)
C 45307 Swap DE and HL - DE = its length
C 45308 Return to the caller
c 45309 Handlers for THATS, HE, IT and THEY
D 45309 Pronouns: HE sets bit 7 and IT/THEY bit 6 of the current parse record's +$19 (used only when Watson or Lestrade weigh a statement: #R$BD7E then takes the statement's subject from #R$9FF2($9FF6), the last person a statement named, for HE, or from #R$9FFA($9FFC), the last thing, for IT/THEY); the first-word versions (#R$B135, #R$B151) also mark the sentence as a statement to a character (#R$A022($A024) = 2). THATS (#R$B0FD) rearranges the parse record's object slots (not traced). Read from the code. CORRECTED: the automatic split had #R$B0FD-#R$B16B($B16C) as data.
D 45309 So pronouns are for statements, not commands. In the emulator TAKE IT and DROP IT after EXAMINE LAMP, and TAKE LAMP AND EXAMINE IT, all gave 'WHAT ?'; HIM and THEM are not in the dictionary at all.
R 45309 Input:E Parse flags (bit 3: a clause has finished)
R 45309 IY The sentence node
R 45309 Output:E Bits 0 to 2, 6 and 7 set
R 45309 IY The node that took the pronoun's words
