D 46085 Once a verb record has matched (#R$B39D): works out from the record (#R$A2BD, #R$A01A($A01D)/#R$A01A($A01E)) and the preposition typed whether the parse node's first or second group of words is the direct object, notes the order at #R$B17D($B182), and copies six bytes of words from the node into the direct-object slot #R$B16D($B172) and the indirect-object slot #R$B175($B178), setting bit 0 of #R$B16D/#R$B16D($B16E) when a slot is not empty. Read from the code.
D 46085 Traced in the emulator 2026-11-08 (breakpoint at #R$B451). TAKE LAMP: no preposition, #R$A01A($A01E) = $40, BC = $0812 - the direct object from the node's first group (+8), the indirect from the second (+18). GIVE WATSON LAMP: no preposition, #R$A01A($A01E) = $61 (bit 5 set), BC = $1208 - the other way round, and the game says 'You give the oil lamp to Watson.' GIVE LAMP TO WATSON: TO matches the node's first group's preposition (+14), #R$A01A($A01D) = $0C, bit 5 flipped, BC = $0812. TALK TO WATSON: TO typed (#R$B185($B188)) but not attached to a group, so #R$A01A($A01D)'s bit 5 ($28) is used as it is: $0812.
D 46085 How it works:
D 46085 1. If the pattern matched with its middle words swapped (A = 1), swap the two prepositions at #R$B185($B188) and #R$B185($B18A).
D 46085 2. Gather the verb's flags (#R$A2BD).
D 46085 3. With no preposition typed, bit 5 of #R$A01A($A01E) decides the order of the two noun phrases (GIVE WATSON LAMP).
D 46085 4. Otherwise see which of the node's two groups the preposition belongs to (+14 or +16) and put each group in the matching slot.
