b 46974 $B6FD's flag
D 46974 Addresses named in the title: #R$B6FD.
D 46974 Cleared by #R$B6FE, tested by #R$B77F.
B 46974,1 Flag
c 46975 Fetch the next word for $B6FD's name match
D 46975 Addresses named in the title: #R$B6FD.
D 46975 Fetch the next word of a message for #R$B6FD's name match. #R$B77E is $FF once the last word of the message has been given out.
D 46975 How it works:
D 46975 1. If #R$B77E is $FF, the message is finished: take the most recent saved call position from the list headed at #R$B6D6 (none left: return Z), unhook it, free its node (#R$A1C7) and carry on reading just after the call.
D 46975 2. Read the next byte. If bit 7 is set it starts a two-byte dictionary reference, which is put in DE. If its class ($70 bits) is $60, $30 or $20 it is the last word of the message, so #R$B77E is set to $FF. Return NZ with the word in DE.
D 46975 3. Below $20 is a control code. Code 2 is a jump: add the 16-bit offset to the position and carry on. Code $0B is a call: make a 2-byte node holding the position after the call, put it on the front of the list at #R$B6D6 and go to the sub-message. Codes $1D to $1F end the text. Other codes are skipped.
D 46975 4. $20 to $5F is a run of packed letters, (byte AND $1F) + 1 of them at five bits each (#R$A990); they are stepped over, not returned.
D 46975 5. $60 and above is one of the common words (#R$A840): return NZ with A = 0.
R 46975 Input:IX The message position
R 46975 Output:F Z when no words are left; NZ when a word was found
R 46975 DE The dictionary reference when A is not 0
R 46975 IX The position after the word
