c 47500 Take the command out of an ASK order
D 47500 The order's verb and objects (+2, +3, +4, resolved by #R$CEDE) become the command; #R$B9FA removes the node.
D 47500 #R$B9AC-#R$B9F9 belong to the remark path of #R$B936: #R$B9CA hands the remark's remaining words back (node +5/+6) one sentence at a time, freeing what has been used. CORRECTED: the automatic split had #R$B9AC-#R$B9F9 as data.
D 47500 Reached from #R$B936 when an actor has been given an order (ASK): the order's verb and objects are turned into the command the actor will run and the order is removed from the list. Orders are issued by ASK instructions in the scripts - for example Lestrade's own script at #R$EA6D, ASK the local policeman TO GO FURIOUSLY TO Tricia's front door - and the block ran in the stored routes (count below). 
D 47500 The tail from #R$B9AC drops an order's sentences when the order cannot be parsed or an ALL has run out, and #R$B9CA hands back the remark's remaining words one sentence at a time. It never ran in the stored routes, so it was provoked on 9 November (tools/experiments/tails_test7.py: orders typed as SAY TO LESTRADE "...", with the player put in Lestrade's room): WAIT and GO EAST - an order he cannot carry out - go #R$B9B4, #R$B9AC, #R$B9CA and he says 'Sorry, I cannot do that now.'; TAKE ALL and DROP ALL, whose ALL has run out, go #R$B9B4, #R$B9BA, #R$B9CA (#R$B9BE-#R$B9C5 on the way); WAIT AND WAIT and GO EAST AND GO EAST drop the first sentence and go round again (#R$B9E9, #R$B9F2, back to #R$B9AC); TAKE ALL AND WAIT goes through #R$B9C7 to try the next sentence; and HELLO AND GOODBYE (which is not an order but speech) goes through #R$B9CA, #R$B9E9, #R$B9F2 and he answers only the first sentence. Every instruction of the tail has now run.
D 47500 Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over the 15 stored routes): the body ran 300 times in 14 of them. Every one of its 45 instructions was executed (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py plus the targeted tests described above).
