c 47719 Watson's reply to something the player says
D 47719 Parses the remark into a statement (#R$BD7E: subject #R$BA13($BA19), relation #R$BA13($BA1A) from the verb's place in #R$994D, object #R$BA1B), then looks for it in Watson's own list at #R$9A7D (empty: $FF at once) and in the case table (#R$BD51). A statement about the case is remembered: added to the list at #R$9FF2($9FF4) (5-byte heap nodes holding the three bytes) unless already there. Watson then answers 'THAT IS BRILLIANT HOLMES.' (#R$6F54) or, by chance, asks whether there is 'SOME CONNECTION BETWEEN THE ... AND ...' two things (#R$6F62, using #R$BD06); a statement with flags bit 5 set (the four 'Tricia Fender / Phipps KILLED ...' ones) gets a different answer.
D 47719 Confirmed in the emulator: SAY TO WATSON "MRS BROWN KILLED HERSELF" at the start gets a reply and the list at #R$9FF2($9FF4) gains a node 5E 05 60 (Mrs Brown / KILLED / herself); "PHIPPS HAS PLANS" gets a reply and a node 06 03 61. The reply is one of two wordings that alternated in the runs of 9 November, whichever statement was said: 'Watson remarks " That is brilliant Holmes, I do not know how you do it. "' and 'Watson says " That is brilliant Holmes. "' (the earlier version of this note tied each wording to a statement; that was wrong). "THE LAMP IS ON", which is not about the case, gets 'Watson says " What "'; "THE LAMP IS OPEN" gets no reply at all.
D 47719 .
D 47719 Line comments added from reading the code. The match kinds 1-4 stored in #R$BA60 are named from the branches (#R$BD4B hit, #R$BD51 hit, and the two dice rolls under 50 and 70); what #R$BA70 carry and #R$BA73 zero from #R$BD7E mean was not traced.
