c 48578 Turn the player's remark into a statement
D 48578 The relation is the position of the remark's verb in the list at #R$994D; the subject and object are the first two things named (#R$BE3C, #R$BE4B), each copied as a 6-byte entry into #R$9FF2($9FF6) (people) or #R$9FFA($9FFC) (things) by #R$BE22. Carry set if the remark names more than that. Read from the code.
D 48578 #R$BE22 copies thing A's 6-byte entry to #R$9FF2($9FF6) (a person, +5 bit 6) or #R$9FFA($9FFC); #R$BE3C and #R$BE4B step through the parser's object slots at #R$B16D($B172) and #R$B175($B178). CORRECTED: the automatic split had #R$BE19($BE1A)-#R$BE59 as data.
D 48578 How it works:
D 48578 1. Start both searches at the top of the thing index (#R$D04F).
D 48578 2. Look the remark's verb word up in the list of relation words at #R$994D. Its position in that list (counting from 1) is the statement's relation. If it is not there the remark is not a statement: return Z.
D 48578 3. Allow things that are not in the room to be named (#R$A032($A038) = 2).
D 48578 4. Find the thing that matches the subject words (#R$BE3C). None means it is not a statement. A second match means the subject is ambiguous: return carry. Otherwise remember it and copy its six-byte entry to #R$9FF2($9FF6) if it is a person or #R$9FFA($9FFC) if not (#R$BE22), so that HE, IT and THEY can refer to it later.
D 48578 5. Do the same for the object words (#R$BE4B).
D 48578 6. Return NZ: the statement's subject, relation and object are in #R$BA13($BA19), #R$BA13($BA1A) and #R$BA1B.
R 48578 Input:$BA17 The remark's verb word
R 48578 Output:F NZ if it made a statement, Z if it is not one
R 48578 Carry set if a name was ambiguous
