c 49113 Advance the clock by one minute
D 49113 Counts the minutes since the start (#R$9FDD), then the minute (#R$9FDF); at 60 the hour (#R$9FE0) moves on, 12 wrapping to 1; at 12 the 'a'/'p' letter (#R$9FE2) flips (XOR $11 turns $61 into $70 and back), and when it becomes 'a' the day (#R$9FE1) moves on. Read from the code; consistent with the clock the game prints.
D 49113 How it works:
D 49113 1. Count the minute since the start (#R$9FDD) and add one to the minute (#R$9FDF).
D 49113 2. At 60, the minute becomes 0 and the hour (#R$9FE0) goes up; 13 becomes 1.
D 49113 3. When the hour reaches 12, am and pm swap; going to am starts the next day (#R$9FE1).
R 49113 Output:The minute counter, the minute, hour, am/pm and day moved on by one minute
C 49113 Read minutes since the start (#R$9FDD) into HL
C 49116 Step HL on to the next byte
C 49117 Store HL in minutes since the start (#R$9FDD)
C 49120 Point HL at the minute (#R$9FDF)
C 49123 Add one to the byte at the minute (#R$9FDF)
C 49124 Read the byte at the minute (#R$9FDF) into A
C 49125 Compare A with $3C - ...reached 60?
C 49127 If A differs from $3C, return
C 49128 Yes: back to 0
C 49130 Point HL at the hour, 1-12 (#R$9FE0)
C 49133 Add one to the byte at the hour, 1-12 (#R$9FE0)
C 49134 Read the byte at the hour, 1-12 (#R$9FE0) into A
C 49135 Compare A with $0D - ...13 becomes 1
C 49137 If A differs from $0D, skip ahead to #R$BFF6
C 49139 Store $01 at the address HL points to
C 49141 Return to the caller
C 49142 Compare A with $0C - Reached 12?
C 49144 If A differs from $0C, return
C 49145 Read 'a' or 'p' (#R$9FE2) into A - Then 'a' and 'p' swap
C 49148 Flip in A the bits that are set in $11
C 49150 Store A in 'a' or 'p' (#R$9FE2)
C 49153 Compare A with $61 - Now 'a'?
C 49155 If A differs from $61, return
C 49156 Read the day (#R$9FE1) into A - Then it is the next day
C 49159 Add one to A
C 49160 Store A in the day (#R$9FE1)
C 49163 Return to the caller
c 49164 What part of the day is it? (A = 1-5)
D 49164 1 night (8pm to 5:59am), 5 dawn (the 6am hour), 4 day (7am to 12:59pm), 3 afternoon (1pm to 6:59pm), 2 dusk (the 7pm hour). Read from the code; #R$D078 treats 1 as dark.
D 49164 The five parts, from the code: 1 night (8pm to 5:59am), 2 the 7pm hour, 3 afternoon (1pm to 6:59pm), 4 morning (7am to 12:59pm), 5 the 6am hour. The message codes $17, $16, $15, $14 and $0F show text only in parts 1-5 respectively (#R$A68C).
D 49164 How it works:
D 49164 1. In the morning (am), 12 midnight to 5:59 is night (1), 6am is dawn (5) and 7 to 11am is morning (4).
D 49164 2. In the afternoon (pm), 12 noon is morning (4), 1 to 6pm is afternoon (3), 7pm is evening (2) and 8pm onwards is night (1).
R 49164 Output:A The part of the day: 1 night, 2 evening, 3 afternoon, 4 morning, 5 dawn
C 49164 Read 'a' or 'p' (#R$9FE2) into A - am or pm?
