c 49611 Print a message: plain ASCII, last byte has bit 7 set
D 49611 A short, self-contained loop: read a byte, strip bit 7, print it (via the gated entry at #R$C1FD), then check whether the *original* byte (before stripping) had bit 7 set - if so, that was the last character of the message and the loop stops; otherwise it advances and repeats.
D 49611 This is genuinely real code - confirmed by finding all four of its actual callers (#R$C070, #R$C09C, #R$C0EA, #R$C1C0), not assumed from reading alone. What it is NOT, also confirmed rather than assumed: the path room descriptions or parser responses take. Breakpointing this address directly during a real `LOOK` command in the emulator produced zero hits. Its callers, and what kind of message actually goes through this simpler, uncompressed format, haven't been identified yet - see source/world_model_notes.md.
D 49611 Its callers turn out to be the save and load routines: the tape messages at #R$C0A5-$C0DE ('REWIND TAPE.', 'TAPE ERROR.', 'PLAY SAVED GAME TAPE TO BE LOADED') are in this form, and #R$C1C0 prints the ROM's own tape messages through it.
R 49611 Input:HL A message whose last byte has bit 7 set
R 49611 Output:The message printed
R 49611 HL At the last byte
C 49611 A = this message byte, with the terminator bit stripped
C 49612 Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 of A (mask $7F)
C 49614 Print it
C 49617 Did the ORIGINAL byte have the terminator bit set?
C 49619 ...then that was the last character - stop
C 49620 Else: next byte
C 49621 Jump to #R$C1CB
C 49623 Return to the caller
C 49624 Load HL with $13C5 - HL = $13C5; (#R$C1C0); then on to #R$A0EB
C 49627 Call the routine "Print a prompt at HL, wait for a key, newline" (#R$C1C0)
C 49630 Go back to #R$A0EB
c 49633 Print gatekeeper: check two flags, then dispatch
D 49633 Checks #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C) first (a flag this project has not fully explained - 'printer echo' is a guess, not confirmed - but which is never zero in this project's emulator, since there's no real ROM to set it, meaning some code paths that check it will always take their 'disabled' branch here); if it's zero, returns immediately, printing nothing.
D 49633 If #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C) is set, checks #R$A022($A027) (the same flag this project has found gating the shared echo/word-buffer routine at #R$A9B7): when #R$A022($A027) is zero, jumps straight to #R$C2BA to print the character normally; when #R$A022($A027) is set, falls through into a case-folding and quote-tracking region (around #R$AA11($AA12)-#R$AA4F($AA50), not fully traced) that eventually calls back into #R$C2BA anyway.
D 49633 Confirmed as the real entry point (correcting an earlier, prose-only attempt at this same routine that guessed a slightly wrong start address from a misaligned disassembly window) by finding two genuine, independent call targets landing exactly here: #R$AA11 (`JP #R$C1E1`) and #R$AA41 (`CALL #R$C1E1`).
R 49633 Input:A The character
R 49633 Output:The character printed in the command window or the text window, as the flags $5C3C and $A027 say
C 49633 Save the character to print
C 49634 Is output enabled at all right now?
C 49637 Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears carry)
C 49638 If A is not zero, skip ahead to #R$C1EA
C 49640 No: print nothing
C 49641 Return to the caller
C 49642 Are we in the other, quote/case-folding mode?
C 49645 Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears carry)
C 49646 If A is not zero, skip ahead to the routine "Echo a typed character in the command window" (#R$C1FC)
C 49648 No: print the character the plain way
C 49649 Jump on to the routine "Print one character (or handle a newline) at the..." (#R$C2BA)
b 49652 Command-window state
D 49652 The bottom window, where the player's typing is echoed. #R$C1F4: characters left on the line (36 to a line: the font is 7 pixels wide); #R$C1F6/#R$C1F6($C1F7): the screen address being written; #R$C1FB: the pixel offset within the byte. #R$C1F5 is the text window's own characters-left count, and #R$C1F8-#R$C1F8($C1FA) its print position and pixel shift. Read from the code.
