B 49653,1 Line count (see #R$C306)
W 49654,2 Screen address
B 49656,3 Not identified
B 49659,1 Pixel offset
c 49660 Echo a typed character in the command window
D 49660 Letters are shown in capitals (#R$C240); ENTER becomes a space and a new line, DELETE steps back 7 pixels (#R$C249, #R$C267); at the end of a line the window scrolls (#R$C271). A '+' is drawn after the character as the cursor - the '+' at the bottom of the screen in the emulator screenshot (Pictures page).
D 49660 How it works:
D 49660 1. ENTER prints a space over the cursor and starts a new line; DELETE rubs the last character out (#R$C249).
D 49660 2. Anything else is shown in capitals (#R$C240) and printed (#R$C388).
D 49660 3. When the line is full, or after ENTER, the window scrolls up (#R$C271) and 36 characters are available again.
D 49660 4. Save the characters left, pixel shift and print position. Draw a '+' cursor after the character.
R 49660 Input:A The character typed (ENTER $0D, DELETE $08, anything else)
R 49660 Output:All registers kept
R 49660 The character shown in the command window
