c 49850 Print one character (or handle a newline) at the cursor
D 49850 The routine PLOT-CHAR's caller: restores the persisted cursor position/column-shift from #R$C1F8/#R$C1F8($C1FA), and either plots the passed-in character directly (calling PLOT-CHAR via #R$C2EE), or, if the character is a carriage return ($0D), counts the line in #R$A012($A019), waits for a key after fifteen (#R$C306), and scrolls the window up (#R$C316) to print the next line at the start of row 17. A character that fills the line (36 to a line, 18 beside a picture: #R$C1F5) is followed by a newline of its own.
D 49850 This, plus PLOT-CHAR and the page-wait wrapper at #R$C306, forms the game's whole low-level text-output path: every piece of printed text - room descriptions, parser responses, dialogue - goes through here one character at a time.
D 49850 CONFIRMED, not just asserted: an earlier pass in this project breakpointed this exact address during a real LOOK command and got zero hits, which was written up (honestly) as an unresolved worry rather than a disproof. Re-run later with the bug in that test fixed, the same breakpoint produces 170 real hits during a single LOOK, with a real, traceable caller chain leading back through the print gatekeeper at #R$C1E1 - see world_model_notes.md's 'room-text print path is resolved' section for the full account, including what the original bug was.
D 49850 How it works:
D 49850 1. Pick up the text print position (#R$C1F8) and pixel shift (#R$C1F8($C1FA)).
D 49850 2. A character other than ENTER is printed (#R$C388); if the line is then full a new line is started.
D 49850 3. A new line after fifteen lines of text (#R$A012($A019)) waits for a key (#R$C306) first. Scroll the text up a row (#R$C316), and start the new line with 36 characters (18 beside a picture).
R 49850 Input:A The character (or $0D for a new line)
R 49850 Output:The character printed at the text cursor; registers kept
