C 50055 Return to the caller
c 50056 PLOT-CHAR: draw one character glyph at HL, shifted by C
D 50056 The genuine low-level character printer. Computes the source glyph address as A*8 + $3C00 - which is exactly the real 128K/48K ROM's own character-bitmap layout (space, code 32, sits at $3D00 = $3C00 + 32*8), confirming A is a plain ASCII code here, not some internal token.
D 50056 For each of the glyph's 8 pixel rows, shifts the byte right by C bits (carrying into the next screen byte, via the 'or c' merge at #R$C3B0/#R$C3CD) so that text can be printed at any pixel column, not just 8-pixel-aligned ones - this is why the routine is more involved than a plain 1:1 glyph copy.
D 50056 This has no real ROM font table to read from in this project's emulator harness (see harness_notes.md), so glyphs render as blank pixels there - but the routine still faithfully receives the real character codes, which is what matters for recovering message text: hooking A at this address is how every piece of game text quoted in this disassembly (the opening room description, parser responses, etc.) was actually captured, per message-rendering.md's guidance to call the real printer rather than hand-decode bytes.
R 50056 Input:A The character
R 50056 HL The screen address
R 50056 C The pixel shift
R 50056 Output:The glyph drawn; all registers kept
