c 50197 GET-KEY, but block until a key is actually pressed
D 50197 A thin blocking wrapper: repeatedly calls the real GET-KEY at #R$C41F, ignoring the 'no key' result, until bit 7 of the return value is set - then masks that bit off and returns the plain ASCII code. Used wherever the game needs to pause for 'press a key to continue' (e.g. the page-wait logic in the print-window routine below), as opposed to the non-blocking poll used elsewhere (e.g. in the boot sequence above).
R 50197 Output:A The key pressed (bit 7 cleared)
C 50197 Ask if a key is available
C 50200 Was one?
C 50202 No: keep asking
C 50204 Yes: strip the "a key was pressed" tag, keep just the ASCII
C 50206 Return to the caller
c 50207 GET-KEY: has the player pressed a key?
D 50207 Saves IY/IX/HL/DE/BC, points IY at the standard system-variable area (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3A)($5C3A)), then does EI; HALT; DI - i.e. waits for exactly one interrupt, which is where a real Spectrum's ROM would normally have scanned the keyboard into FLAGS/LAST-K.
D 50207 Checks FLAGS bit 5 ("a new key is waiting"); if not, returns A=0. If a key is waiting, clears that bit, reads LAST-K (the ROM's already-decoded ASCII value), remaps three ROM editing-key codes (an underscore-shaped delete-left key, and two others) onto plainer control codes the game's own line editor expects, calls a small helper at #R$C240 (not yet examined), then sets bit 7 of A before returning - so the caller's contract is: A=0 means no key, A = ASCII|$80 means this key was pressed.
D 50207 Called from three places, all real CALL instructions: #R$A07F($A080) (non-blocking poll during boot), #R$A53A($A53B), and #R$C415($C416) (the wait-for-a-key loop at #R$C415).
D 50207 Confirmed in the emulator: this is the only place in the whole 41,600-byte block that reads FLAGS/LAST-K at all, and there are zero raw `IN A,($FE)`-style port reads anywhere in the block, so this is genuinely the game's one and only keyboard entry point, not just a convenient guess. See harness_notes.md for how this was verified by hooking it directly and recovering legible printed game text.
D 50207 How it works:
D 50207 1. Save the registers and point IY at the system variables.
D 50207 2. Allow one interrupt (EI, HALT) so the keyboard is scanned.
D 50207 3. If no new key is flagged, return 0. Otherwise take the key from #LINK(rom_symbols#a5C08)($5C08), turn the ROM's editing codes into backspace and '[', and put it in capitals.
D 50207 4. Set bit 7 to say a key was pressed.
R 50207 Output:A The key with bit 7 set, or 0 if none
R 50207 All other registers kept
