c 50728 Check the direct object against the action record's requirement
D 50728 The low nibble of an action record's byte 0 is a requirement code for the direct object, copied to #R$A00A($A00F) by #R$C571; the low nibble of byte 1 is the same for the indirect object (#R$C6BE). Returns Z to refuse (#R$C632 is JP #R$DC15). Codes 7-15 are handed to #R$C7A6.
D 50728 0: none. 1 and 2: the object must be a room whose record (#R$D224, the room table at #R$840B) has bit 7 of byte 0 set; 1 also needs #R$D471 to find something. 3: #R$D471 must find something. 4 and 7: anything. 5: a number (#R$A032($A034) = $FE) with bit 7 of the value clear (WAIT UNTIL); 6: the same with bit 7 set (PAY TO). 8: the object must be here (#R$C821). 9: TAKE - here, in exactly one location (#R$C8A7), not a person (#R$C8BE), small enough for the actor to carry (#R$C8C6), and not held by a person (#R$C8AF). 10: here and held by the actor. 11: here and in more than one location - a door or passage. 12: a person, with no visibility check (FOLLOW). 13-15: here and a person.
D 50728 Confirmed in the emulator: TAKE PLAIN DOOR, TAKE WATSON, GO THROUGH PLAIN DOOR and TAKE DISGUISE get 'I SEE NO ... THAT YOU CAN ...'; DROP LAMP when not carried, TALK TO LAMP, GO THROUGH LAMP, FOLLOW LAMP, GIVE LAMP TO WATSON when not carried and a second TAKE LAMP get 'YOU CANNOT ... THE ...'; PAY 5 TO WATSON gets 'DON'T BE SILLY.'
D 50728 After the code check, a record with flags bit 4 has five more header bytes: a byte of test modes and two pairs of masks, tested against the objects' +5 flags by #R$C74E (#R$C691 for the direct object; the indirect object's half is the same at #R$C6BE's end).
