c 51022 Test object C's +5 flags against a record's masks
D 51022 B holds two 2-bit modes and L/H two masks. Mode 0: every mask bit must be set; 1: at least one (or the mask is 0); 2: none may be set; 3: not all may be set (read from the code; modes 1 and 3 were not tried). On failure the offending bits go to #R$CB79 (bit 7 set when a bit was present that should not be) and HL = #R$CB7B, the refusal that names the flag.
D 51022 Confirmed in the emulator: OPEN YOUR DOOR when it is open leaves #R$CB79 = $A0 and prints 'YOUR DOOR IS OPEN'; CLOSE YOUR DOOR when it is closed leaves $20 and prints 'YOUR DOOR IS CLOSED'. The door's +5 goes $80 -> $A0 when opened and back when closed.
R 51022 Input:C An object
R 51022 B Two modes (bits 2-3 for mask L, bits 0-1 for mask H)
R 51022 L, H The masks
R 51022 Output:F NZ if the object's +5 flags pass both tests; Z if not
C 51022 Copy C into A - C = the object's +5 flags
C 51023 Call the routine "Resolve an id (in A) to its full entity record" (#R$D237)
C 51026 Read byte +5 of the record at IX into C
C 51029 Copy B into A - First the mode in bits 2-3 of B with mask L...
C 51030 Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
C 51031 Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
C 51032 Keep only bits 0, 1 of A (mask $03)
C 51034 Copy L into D
C 51035 Call #R$C763 (inside "Test object C's +5 flags against a record's...")
C 51038 (failed: return Z)
C 51039 Copy B into A - ...then the mode in bits 0-1 with mask H
C 51040 Keep only bits 0, 1 of A (mask $03)
C 51042 Copy H into D
C 51043 If any of bits 0, 1 of A was set, skip ahead to #R$C76D - Mode 0: every mask bit must be set
C 51045 Copy D into A
C 51046 Keep only the bits of A that are set in C
C 51047 Compare A with D
C 51048 If A equals D, go back to #R$C62F
C 51051 (missing: note the bits that are not)
C 51053 Compare A with $01 - Mode 1: at least one (or no mask at all)
C 51055 If A differs from $01, skip ahead to #R$C77B
C 51057 Copy D into A
C 51058 Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears carry)
C 51059 If A is zero, go back to #R$C768
C 51061 Keep only the bits of A that are set in C
C 51062 If none of those bits of A are set, skip ahead to #R$C795
C 51064 Go back to #R$C62F
C 51067 Compare A with $02 - Mode 2: none may be set
C 51069 If A differs from $02, skip ahead to #R$C788
C 51071 Copy D into A
C 51072 Keep only the bits of A that are set in C
C 51073 If none of those bits of A are set, go back to #R$C62F
C 51076 Set bit 7 of A - (note them, with bit 7: present but should not be)
C 51078 Skip ahead to #R$C797
C 51080 Copy D into A - Mode 3: not all may be set
C 51081 Keep only the bits of A that are set in C
C 51082 Compare A with D
C 51083 If A differs from D, go back to #R$C62F
C 51086 Set bit 7 of A
C 51088 Skip ahead to #R$C797
C 51090 Flip in A the bits that are set in $FF
C 51092 Keep only the bits of A that are set in D
C 51093 Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 of A (mask $7F)
C 51095 Failed: the bits go to #R$CB79
C 51098 Read #R$A00A($A00B) in "Game variables" into A - ...but the failure is ignored while #R$A00A($A00B) is clear
C 51101 Test A for zero (this leaves A as it was and clears carry)
C 51102 If A is zero, go back to #R$C62F
C 51105 Point HL at "Refuse by naming the flag that failed: "THE ......" (#R$CB7B) - Z, with HL = the refusal that names the flag (#R$CB7B)
C 51108 Set A to zero, which sets the zero flag for the caller
C 51109 Return to the caller
c 51110 Check object C against requirement code A (7-15)
D 51110 The second half of the requirement checks; see #R$C628 for the codes. Each refusal goes to #R$C632, each pass to #R$C62F.
D 51110 How it works:
D 51110 1. Mask the code to its low nibble. Codes 7 to 15 split into six groups; each ends by jumping to the pass at #R$C62F or the refusal at #R$C632.
D 51110 2. Code 7: anything will do. Code 8: the object must be in the actor's room (#R$C821).
D 51110 3. Code 9 (TAKE): in the room; in exactly one place (#R$C8A7), so doors and the like are out; not a person (#R$C8BE); of a size the actor can carry (#R$C8C6); and not already held by a person (#R$C8AF).
D 51110 4. Code 10: in the room and held by the actor (#R$D275). Code 11: in the room and in more than one place, which is what a door is. Code 12: a person anywhere. Codes 13 to 15: a person who is in the room.
R 51110 Input:A The requirement code (7-15)
R 51110 C The object's id
R 51110 IY The action record
R 51110 Output:F NZ if the object passes, Z if it is refused
C 51110 Keep only bits 0, 1, 2, 3 of A (mask $0F)
C 51112 Compare A with $07 - 7: anything at all
C 51114 If A equals $07, go back to #R$C62F
C 51117 Compare A with $08 - 8: it must be here (#R$C821)
C 51119 If A differs from $08, skip ahead to #R$C7BB
C 51121 Copy C into A
C 51122 Call the routine "Can the current actor (#R$A032($A036)) see thing A?" (#R$C821)
C 51125 If the zero flag is set, go back to #R$C632
C 51128 Go back to #R$C62F
C 51131 Compare A with $09 - 9: something to take - here,
C 51133 If A differs from $09, skip ahead to #R$C7E4
C 51135 Copy C into A
C 51136 Call the routine "Can the current actor (#R$A032($A036)) see thing A?" (#R$C821)
C 51139 If the zero flag is set, go back to #R$C632
C 51142 Copy C into A - in only one place (#R$C8A7),
C 51143 Call the routine "How many locations has object A? (Z if exactly one)" (#R$C8A7)
C 51146 If the zero flag is clear, go back to #R$C632
C 51149 Copy C into A - not a person (#R$C8BE),
C 51150 Call the routine "Is object A a person? (NZ if so)" (#R$C8BE)
C 51153 If the zero flag is clear, go back to #R$C632
C 51156 Copy C into B - something the actor can carry (#R$C8C6),
C 51157 Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A
C 51160 Call the routine "Can the actor carry object A as well as what it holds?" (#R$C8C6)
C 51163 If the zero flag is set, go back to #R$C632
C 51166 Copy C into A - and then whether a person holds it (#R$C8AF, on at #R$C768)
C 51167 Call the routine "Is object A held, at any depth, by a person?" (#R$C8AF)
C 51170 Go back to #R$C768
C 51172 Compare A with $0A - 10: here and carried by the actor (#R$D275)
C 51174 If A differs from $0A, skip ahead to #R$C7F9
C 51176 Copy C into A
C 51177 Call the routine "Can the current actor (#R$A032($A036)) see thing A?" (#R$C821)
C 51180 If the zero flag is set, go back to #R$C632
C 51183 Copy C into A
C 51184 Call the routine "Is thing A carried by the actor, perhaps inside..." (#R$D275)
C 51187 If the carry flag is clear, go back to #R$C632
C 51190 Go back to #R$C62F
C 51193 Compare A with $0B - 11: here and in more than one place (a door, say)
C 51195 If A differs from $0B, skip ahead to #R$C80A
C 51197 Copy C into A
C 51198 Call the routine "Can the current actor (#R$A032($A036)) see thing A?" (#R$C821)
C 51201 If the zero flag is set, go back to #R$C632
C 51204 Copy C into A
C 51205 Call the routine "How many locations has object A? (Z if exactly one)" (#R$C8A7)
C 51208 Go back to #R$C7B5
C 51210 Compare A with $0C - 12: a person, anywhere
C 51212 If A differs from $0C, skip ahead to #R$C814
C 51214 Copy C into A
C 51215 Call the routine "Is object A a person? (NZ if so)" (#R$C8BE)
C 51218 Go back to #R$C7B5
C 51220 Copy C into A - 13-15: a person who is here
C 51221 Call the routine "Can the current actor (#R$A032($A036)) see thing A?" (#R$C821)
C 51224 If the zero flag is set, go back to #R$C632
C 51227 Copy C into A
C 51228 Call the routine "Is object A a person? (NZ if so)" (#R$C8BE)
C 51231 Go back to #R$C7B5
c 51233 Can the current actor ($A036) see thing A?
D 51233 Addresses named in the title: #R$A032($A036).
D 51233 NZ if it can. Yes when the thing is what the actor is in or on (the root of the actor's own containment chain, #R$C863); no when the thing's +5 bit 7 is clear (the underground trains, objects 37-41); yes when the thing's chain ends at the actor (carried, or in something open the actor carries) or at the same holder as the actor's; and when neither is held by anything, yes if the actor's room (#R$A00A, set by #R$CC29) is one of the thing's rooms (+0 is how many, from +15 on).
D 51233 CORRECTED 2026-11-08: the test is from the current actor's point of view, not the player's. Confirmed in the emulator: during one WAIT at the start, #R$CC64 asks it about the player (thing 0) for every actor, and it answers yes only for Watson (actor 3, room 8) and the player himself, both in the sitting room; Mrs Hudson (actor 2, room 10) and everyone else get no.
D 51233 Resolves the id in A via #R$D237 and the current id in #R$A032($A036) via #R$C863 (the containment-chain walker above), and compares them - essentially 'does this candidate's ultimate container match the thing in #R$A032($A036) (usually the player, or the room being checked)'. Called from 12 real, confirmed sites across the code, among them the already-documented #R$CC64 (which uses the result to decide whether to enable output for an NPC's turn), which independently confirms this is a genuine 'is this visible / present here' test rather than a guess from its name alone.
D 51233 Confirmed with a real id catalog, not just plausible-sounding prose: breakpointing this during known commands shows a stable, repeated id for objects that are actually present (LAMP -> 22, the two armchairs -> 23 and 24, WATSON -> 3), and only a handful of never-repeated ids for a typed object that isn't there (PIPE - not carried or in the room - gives up after 5 hits with no stable id). id 3 for WATSON independently confirms a guess this project made several rounds earlier (that actor id 3, whose room this project separately found to match the player's, was 'very plausibly Watson') - see source/world_model_notes.md for the full account, including two lower-confidence results (SOFA, MONEY) that don't fit the clean pattern as neatly.
D 51233 How it works:
D 51233 1. The thing is seen if it is what the actor is in or on, or the actor's own container.
D 51233 2. A thing with +5 bit 7 clear cannot be seen.
D 51233 3. Things held by the actor, or with the same top-level holder, can be seen; things with different holders cannot.
D 51233 4. If neither is held, the thing can be seen when it is in the actor's room.
R 51233 Input:A A thing number
R 51233 Output:F NZ if the actor ($A036) can see it; Z if not
