c 51310 Follow an object's containment chain to its root
D 51310 Resolves an id via #R$D237, then repeatedly follows a 'next id' held at the resolved record's own offset +1 (not a memory address - another id, resolved the same way each time), continuing while bit 5 or bit 3 of the new record's offset +5 is set, and stopping either when +1 is $FF or when bit 7 of +5 is clear. Read as 'find what ultimately contains this thing, walking up through however many levels of nesting' - a room contains a container, which contains an item, which an actor might be carrying.
D 51310 Bit 5 of offset +5 is now confirmed: it's 'is this thing open' (see #R$DE9D below, a shared OPEN/CLOSE implementation that sets and clears exactly this bit). That makes this routine's bit-5 check read naturally: keep following the chain (i.e. treat what's inside as reachable) only while each container along the way is open. Bits 3 and 7 are still not confirmed.
D 51310 How it works:
D 51310 1. Start with the thing's holder (+1). If it is $FF nothing holds it, and the answer is the last holder reached.
D 51310 2. Otherwise move up to the holder's record, but only climb further when the holder's +5 bit 7 is set and it also has bit 5 or bit 3 set (it lets what is inside be seen).
D 51310 3. Return the highest holder reached.
R 51310 Input:IX A thing's record
R 51310 Output:A The highest holder reached, $FF if none
R 51310 IX Kept
C 51310 Save IX on the stack - A = the holder number so far ($FF at the start); IX = the thing
