c 51552 Look up a numbered entry in the table at $AA71
D 51552 Addresses named in the title: #R$AA71.
D 51552 Given a number in (IX+1), computes #R$AA71 + (that number - 1)*2 and reads a 16-bit value from there - a plain, direct-indexed table lookup, not a search.
D 51552 Confirmed real: dumped the table's first 20 entries and every one is a valid address inside the loaded 'p' block. They fall into two distinct bands - roughly $9Axx and $Exxx - and checking one entry from each band shows the $9Axx one is genuine executable code (a real CALL and a real JP Z appear at the very start of it, 2 bytes in - the first 2 bytes look like they double as a small header), while the $Exxx ones checked so far don't look like straight code - more likely a smaller, more compact per-entry record of some kind. Not yet resolved which; see source/world_model_notes.md.
D 51552 IMPORTANT, and corrected from an earlier draft of this comment: (IX+1) is NOT simply 'which verb the player typed'. Breakpointing this address during real play shows the exact same value (11, with IX fixed at #R$8F06($8F08)) firing after LOOK, INVENTORY, and TAKE alike - three different verbs - but never after an unrecognised command, and OPEN additionally fires a completely different value (6, IX=#R$90E4($90E5)) three times *before* that same 11/#R$8F06($8F08) check. The cleaner reading, not yet fully confirmed: IX points at whichever record is currently being tested (a fixed, generic one at #R$8F06($8F08) that every successfully-parsed command checks at the end, plus an object-specific one - #R$90E4($90E5), very possibly the door object itself - tried while resolving which specific thing OPEN applies to), and the number at +1 is that record's own 'primary action' field - exactly discovery.md's 'an action-number byte immediately precedes this address' pattern, just keyed per-record rather than globally per-verb. See source/world_model_notes.md for the tests behind this.
R 51552 Input:IX A record whose +1 is a number
R 51552 Output:HL The action record at entry (number - 1) of the table at $AA71
C 51552 The number to look up (meaning depends on IX - see prose)
