c 51647 Run an action record (HL = the record)
D 51647 Action records are a small data format that every verb's behaviour is written in. Byte 0 is a flags byte, byte 1 holds a value whose high nibble is stored at #R$A01A($A01F) (meaning not confirmed - see below). UPDATED: the low nibbles of bytes 0 and 1 are requirement codes for the direct and indirect objects, checked by #R$C628 and #R$C6BE, and the rest depends on the flags:
D 51647 bit 5: before anything else, refuse (HL = #R$CBBB) if #R$D078 says so; bit 6: bytes 2-3 are the address of a pre-check routine, run through #R$CB34 - it returns Z to refuse; bit 4: five more header bytes follow (UPDATED: flag tests on the objects' +5 bits, done by #R$C74E - see #R$C628); bit 7: stop after the checks with carry set, without running the body - UPDATED: the bytes after such a header are a plan script, which the caller (#R$C9A7) makes the actor run as a sub-script. The executable body starts after the header: record + 2, + 2 more if bit 6, + 5 more if bit 4. #R$CA64 runs the body through the dispatcher at #R$CB2C.
D 51647 Every flag rule above was read from this routine and then checked against the records it runs: the computed body address lands on sensible code for all the records examined (e.g. SIT IN's $78 header puts the body at record + 9, #R$E413).
D 51647 Tested and NOT confirmed: that the byte-1 value is the number of minutes the action takes. The clock does move by 5 for one WAIT (value 5) and 2 for GET OUT (value 2), but another WAIT moved it by 1 and SIT IN (value 2) by 1.
D 51647 UPDATED 2026-11-07: #R$A01A($A01F) is how many minutes the action takes - the main loop (#R$A064) adds it to the actor's next-turn time. Confirmed in the emulator by watching the player's next-turn time (actor table +6/+7): WAIT (WAIT HERE, byte 1 $50) moves it on by 5, TAKE LAMP (#R$DCC1, $10) and EXAMINE LAMP (#R$DF40, $10) by 1 each. Records run inside sub-scripts and characters' interruptions make the clock look less regular from the player's side, which is probably what threw the earlier test.
D 51647 How it works:
D 51647 1. Refuse if it is too dark (#R$D078) and the record cares about that.
D 51647 2. Check the direct object (#R$C628) and the indirect object (#R$C6BE) against the record.
D 51647 3. If bit 6 of the record is set, run its pre-check routine; if that refuses, stop.
D 51647 4. Otherwise take the high nibble of byte 1 as the action's length (#R$A01A($A01F)) and run the body, narrating the action first and describing any change.
R 51647 Input:HL An action record
R 51647 Output:F Z with HL = the refusal handler if refused; NZ if the record's checks pass; carry set if it is a script
C 51647 IY = the action record
C 51648 Restore IY from the stack
C 51650 Is the action ruled out by #R$D078?
C 51653 No: carry on
C 51655 Yes - but only refuse if this record says that matters
C 51658 Isolate bit 5 of A (mask $20)
C 51660 If bit 5 of A was clear, skip ahead to #R$C9D3
C 51662 HL = the refusal handler
C 51665 Refuse (Z)
C 51666 Return to the caller
C 51667 Check the direct object against the record (#R$C628); refuse if it fails
C 51670 If the zero flag is set, go back to #R$C9D1
C 51672 Check the indirect object (#R$C6BE); refuse if it fails
C 51675 If the zero flag is set, go back to #R$C9D1
C 51677 Does the record have a pre-check routine?
C 51681 If bit 6 of the first byte of the record at IY is clear, skip ahead to #R$C9F2
C 51683 Yes: HL = its address...
C 51686 Read byte +3 of the record at IY into H
