c 52012 Dispatch to a handler routine via a computed jump
D 52012 Loads IX and IY from a saved 'action context' (#R$CB28/#R$CB2A), calls through to a plain `JP (HL)` at #R$CB40, then stores whatever IX/IY the handler left behind back into #R$CB28/#R$CB2A. So IX/IY persist from one dispatched handler to the next, like a pair of global registers - the caller's own IX/IY are not protected. #R$CB34 is a second entry point that skips the load (used by the action-record interpreter at #R$C9EB to run a record's pre-check routine with the current IX/IY).
D 52012 CORRECTED: an earlier version of this entry (and its line comments) said the routine 'saves the caller's IX/IY and restores them afterwards' - exactly backwards. `LD IX,(#R$CB28)` loads IX from memory; `LD (#R$CB28),IX` stores it. Caught on a re-read with a working disassembler, per verification.md item 5 ('re-derive register roles from a fresh trace').
D 52012 Confirmed as a genuinely real, deliberate mechanism (not misdecoded data) by finding its one real caller (#R$CA64, via the literal bytes CD 2C CB) and by the coherence of what surrounds that call - it sits in what looks like the main per-turn command loop, immediately followed by a check for whether the player's room changed as a result of whatever just got dispatched to.
D 52012 UPDATED: what loads HL before this call is now fully traced, correcting the guess in the previous paragraph (that it would be 'the verb/action dispatch table' - it isn't). #R$CA64 is itself called, once per candidate, from the same per-candidate walk this project found much earlier via #R$C9C0 (CORRECTED: not a fixed table but the five RUN steps of the player's plan script at #R$9921, then the command itself); HL comes from that candidate's own record fields (the (IY+2)/(IY+3) pattern found at #R$C9E3), not from the #R$AA71 table. See source/world_model_notes.md's 'unifying several previously-separate threads' section for the full, now-closed trace.
