b 52235 Bytes for the actor's turn ($CC37)
D 52235 Addresses named in the title: #R$CC37.
D 52235 Cleared by #R$CC38, read at #R$CC8C($CC8D), its address used at #R$CEB8($CEB9). Read from the code.
B 52235,3 Bytes for the actor's turn (#R$CC37)
c 52238 Find the actor record whose id matches C
D 52238 A plain linear search over the sentinel-terminated array of 8-byte actor/character records at #R$9860 (see the boot sequence in overlay_01_boot.py, where this table was first found): given an id in C, walks the table 8 bytes at a time until either field +0 matches (found - IY left pointing at that record) or field +0 is $FF (not found).
D 52238 This is the routine that turns the loose 'we saw code walk a table shaped like this' observation from the boot sequence into a confirmed, general-purpose accessor: it's called from four separate places in the code (#R$CC3D, #R$DDA3, #R$E03E, #R$E300 - all genuine CALL instructions, found by searching for the literal bytes CD 0E CC), which is real cross-reference evidence this is a widely-used 'look up an actor' primitive, not a one-off.
R 52238 Input:A An actor number
R 52238 Output:IY The actor's record, or the $FF end marker if there is none
R 52238 BC, DE Kept
C 52238 Save DE on the stack - Record size is 8 bytes
C 52239 Save BC on the stack
C 52240 Copy A into C
C 52241 Load DE with $0008
C 52244 IY -> first actor record
C 52248 This record's id byte...
C 52251 ...is it the one we want?
C 52252 If A equals C, skip ahead to #R$CC26
C 52254 ...or have we hit the end-of-table sentinel?
C 52256 If A equals $FF, skip ahead to #R$CC26
C 52258 Neither: move to the next record
C 52260 Go back to #R$CC18
C 52262 Restore BC, DE and return
C 52263 Restore DE from the stack
C 52264 Return to the caller
