D 52265 #R$A012($A017) = the record of the actor in #R$A032($A036) and #R$A00A = its room (#R$D495). Read from the code.
R 52265 Input:$A036 The actor
R 52265 Output:$A017 The actor's record
R 52265 $A00A The actor's room
R 52265 IX The record
C 52265 Read #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables" into A - The current actor (#R$A032($A036)): its thing record (#R$D495)
C 52268 Call the routine "GET-ACTOR-ROOM: what room is actor A currently in?" (#R$D495)
C 52271 Store IX in #R$A012($A017) in "Game variables" - Keep the record at #R$A012($A017) and the room (A) at #R$A00A
C 52275 Store A in the current actor's room (#R$A00A)
C 52278 Return to the caller
c 52279 Start an actor's turn
D 52279 UPDATED 2026-11-07, from the main loop (#R$A064) and the plan interpreter: clears the failure count (#R$CC0B), finds the actor's record (IY, saved at #R$9FDB, id at #R$A032($A036)), sets #R$CC0B($CC0D) when an order or remark is waiting for it (#R$B91F), works out its room and the player's (#R$CC29, #R$D045), and switches output on (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C) = 1) only if the player can see the actor (#R$C821) - which is why you only read what characters do when they are with you. #R$A01A($A01F) starts at 0 (1 if the actor is not found). Consistent with the emulator: Lestrade hailing a cab and giving the cabbie his orders appears in the output only in the runs where the player was moved to his room.
D 52279 Given an actor id in A: clears a flag at #R$CC0B, resolves the id to its full state record via the already-confirmed #R$CC0E, stashes that record's pointer at #R$9FDB and the id itself at #R$A032($A036), calls #R$B91F (not yet examined), then gets this actor's current room (via the already-confirmed #R$CC29/#R$D495 chain) and the player's own current room (via the already-confirmed #R$D045). On failure to resolve the id, clears #R$A01A($A01F) and returns early instead.
D 52279 Confirmed real and genuinely on the per-turn NPC-processing path: this is called (via #R$CC5A) from #R$CC78, which is itself called from inside the actor-table walk that overlay_01_boot.py originally described as boot-only - see world_model_notes.md for how that walk was shown to be reentrant, and the correction now recorded there and in this project's boot-sequence notes.
D 52279 How it works:
D 52279 1. Clear the failure count (#R$CC0B). Find the actor's record (#R$CC0E); if there is none, make the action take 1 minute and stop.
D 52279 2. Keep the record address (#R$9FDB) and the actor (#R$A032($A036)), and note whether an order or remark is waiting (#R$B91F).
D 52279 3. Set up the actor (#R$CC29) and note the player's room (#R$D045).
D 52279 4. Switch output on only when the player can see the actor (#R$C821). Clear #R$A01A($A01F).
R 52279 Input:A The actor number
R 52279 Output:$A01F 0 (or 1 if the actor is not in the table)
R 52279 IY The actor's record
R 52279 $A036 The actor
R 52279 $5C3C Output on only when the actor is seen by the player
C 52279 Save AF on the stack - Clear the failure count (#R$CC0B)
C 52280 Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
C 52281 Store A in "Bytes for the actor's turn (#R$CC37)" (#R$CC0B)
C 52284 Restore AF from the stack
C 52285 Find the actor's record (#R$CC0E)
C 52288 Compare A with $FF - Not found: #R$A01A($A01F) = 1 and return
C 52290 Load HL with $0001
C 52293 If A equals $FF, jump on to #R$CC74
C 52296 Store IY in the current actor's (#R$9FDB) - Save the record address and the actor's id
C 52300 Store A in #R$A032($A036) in "Game variables"
C 52303 Call the routine "Is an order or a remark waiting for the current actor?" (#R$B91F) - #R$CC0B($CC0D) = 1 if an order or remark is waiting for it (#R$B91F), 0 if not
C 52306 Load A with $00
C 52308 If the zero flag is clear, skip ahead to #R$CC57
C 52310 Add one to A
C 52311 Store A in #R$CC0B($CC0D) in "Bytes for the actor's turn (#R$CC37)"
C 52314 Call the routine "Set up the current actor: its record and room" (#R$CC29) - Set the actor up as the subject (#R$CC29, #R$D045)
C 52317 Call the routine "Note the room the player is in (#R$A002($A009))" (#R$D045)
C 52320 Clear A to zero - Output off (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C) = 0)...
C 52321 Store A in the output-switch byte (TV_FLAG) (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C))
C 52324 Read "The actor table: who acts, and where each is in..." (#R$9860) into A - ...unless the actor is where the player is (#R$C821)
C 52327 Call the routine "Can the current actor (#R$A032($A036)) see thing A?" (#R$C821)
C 52330 If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$CC71
C 52332 Load A with $01
C 52334 Store A in the output-switch byte (TV_FLAG) (#LINK(rom_symbols#a5C3C)($5C3C))
C 52337 Load HL with $0000 - #R$A01A($A01F) = 0
C 52340 Store HL in #R$A01A($A01F) in "Game variables"
C 52343 Return to the caller
c 52344 Run one step of the current actor's plan script
D 52344 Each actor (the 8-byte records at #R$9860: +0 id, +1/+2 script position, +3/+4/+5 saved plan registers) runs a script in a small language. Each instruction starts with one byte: the low nibble is the opcode and the high nibble holds flags. Bit 7: a 2-byte success address follows the operands. Bit 4: a 2-byte failure address follows (after the success address, if both). Bit 5: one-shot - after it succeeds the instruction is overwritten with zeros (#R$CF43), and the fetch loop at #R$CC94 skips zero bytes, so it never runs again - though no script in the game sets bit 5 (all decoded instructions were checked), so this is never used. Bit 6: busy - while a character is on such an instruction it will not do what the player asks of it: #R$B936 copies the instruction's first byte to #R$B934, and #R$CBA7 (used by SIT, GET OUT and the move pre-check #R$DCD2) refuses with 'SORRY, I CANNOT DO THAT NOW.' Confirmed in the emulator (see #R$CBA7).
D 52344 Opcodes (lengths from #R$CF03): 1 DO verb (2 bytes); 2 DO verb dobj iobj (4); 3 RUN record (3); 4 ASK person TO verb dobj iobj (5): an order to another character, queued on the list at #R$9FEE and announced with #R$682A; 5 SAY TO person message (4), via #R$6830; 6 PRINT message (3); 7 RUN record dobj iobj (5); 8 SIGNAL actor (2) and 9 AWAIT actor (2): one actor leaves a token on the #R$9FE7 list for another, which AWAIT consumes; A RETURN FAIL and B RETURN OK (1 each): leave a sub-script; D (2) and F (1) PASS: spend a minute doing nothing; E GOTO address (3). C is not used: #R$CD08 is JR Z,#R$CD08, a jump to itself with Z set, which would hang the game.
D 52344 DO runs a command for the actor exactly as if it had been typed, through #R$C4D1: the verb's own default record, the object's own action list, the requirement codes and the pre-check all apply. Verbs 128-140 exist only for scripts and are looked up in the table at #R$9FAB. RUN skips the verb and runs one action record directly (#R$C591). Argument bytes $F5-$FF are not objects: $FA is the plan's verb (#R$9FD6), $FB the actor itself, $FC and $FD the plan's indirect and direct objects (#R$9FD8, #R$9FD7), $FE a number and $FF nothing (#R$CEDE).
D 52344 Success (#R$CE8F) moves to the success address or the next instruction and returns to the main loop, which runs the next step at once if the action took no time and otherwise ends the actor's turn for as many minutes as it took. Failure (#R$CEB8) moves to the failure address or the next instruction and tries again at once, up to ten times in one turn (#R$CC0B); if the failure address is the instruction itself, the actor simply waits there. So 'DO verb #R$F1D6(136) / 23 [fail -> itself]' means 'wait here until 11pm'.
D 52344 A RUN or DO that reaches a checks-only action record (flags bit 7) calls a sub-script: #R$C9A7 saves a call frame (#R$CF80: actor, return position, objects, plan verb) on the list at #R$9FE9 and points the actor at the script after the record's header. RETURN OK or RETURN FAIL (#R$CFCF) drops the frame and makes the calling instruction succeed or fail.
D 52344 This routine fetches the actor's script position (+1/+2 of its record), skips spent one-shot bytes, and dispatches on the opcode. #R$CCA7-#R$CCC6 is a separate path for an actor who has been given an order (ASK) this turn (#R$CC0B($CC0D), set by #R$CC37 from #R$B91F): the order is carried out first, through #R$B936.
D 52344 Confirmed in the emulator: a breakpoint at #R$CC9B over 106 turns saw 15,038 fetches by 24 actors, every one at an instruction start that tools/plan.py decodes, and opcodes 1-11, 14 and 15 all occurred (12 and 13 never did).
D 52344 Instruction coverage, 9 November: 73 of this routine's 75 instructions were executed over the 15 stored routes, including the order path #R$CCAE-#R$CCC6 (an actor who has been given an ASK order). The two that never ran, #R$CC98 and #R$CC99, skip a zero byte - a spent one-shot instruction - and no script in the game uses one-shot instructions.
D 52344 How it works:
D 52344 1. Set up the actor (#R$CC5A) and load its plan registers (#R$D008). Take its script position from its record.
D 52344 2. If ten steps have failed this turn, give up until next turn (#R$CD8B). Skip any zero bytes (spent one-shot instructions).
D 52344 3. The opcode is the low nibble of the first byte. Codes of 10 and up are control flow (#R$CCF3); the others are actions. If an order was given this turn, turn it into a command (#R$B936) and carry it out first.
D 52344 4. Run the instruction. It succeeds (#R$CE8F), moving to its success address, or fails (#R$CEB8), moving to its failure address, and a one-shot instruction is erased (#R$CF43).
R 52344 Input:A The actor number
R 52344 Output:$A01F The minutes the step took (0 if none)
