w 53338 How a room is entered in words: 'You are ON Baker Street'
D 53338 Indexed by bits 1-3 of the room record's first byte (#R$D0DD): OUTSIDE, INSIDE, IN, ON, AT, NORTH, SOUTH, EAST. The word is written into the message 'YOU ARE IN' at #R$68DD itself (its second word, at #R$68DD($68DE)) before it is printed - self-modifying message data. Confirmed in the emulator in effect: Baker Street is described 'You are on Baker Street.' CORRECTED: the automatic split had this as code.
D 53338 Kinds 5-7 are really the start of the next table: the direction words at #R$D064 (NORTH, SOUTH, EAST, then WEST to DOWN at #R$D06A), which #R$D5FB indexes by direction number. No room uses kinds 0, 1 or 5-7: 65 rooms are IN, 32 ON and 9 AT (counted from the room records).
W 53338,2 Room kind 0: OUTSIDE
W 53340,2 Room kind 1: INSIDE
W 53342,2 Room kind 2: IN
W 53344,2 Room kind 3: ON
W 53346,2 Room kind 4: AT
W 53348,2 Room kind 5: NORTH
W 53350,2 Room kind 6: SOUTH
W 53352,2 Room kind 7: EAST
w 53354 Direction words, continued: WEST to DOWN
D 53354 The direction-word table starts at #R$D064, overlapping the last three room kinds (NORTH, SOUTH, EAST); #R$D5FB reads entry n-1 for direction n. CORRECTED: was 'not identified'.
W 53354,2 Direction 4: WEST
W 53356,2 Direction 5: NORTHEAST
W 53358,2 Direction 6: NORTHWEST
W 53360,2 Direction 7: SOUTHEAST
W 53362,2 Direction 8: SOUTHWEST
W 53364,2 Direction 9: UP
W 53366,2 Direction 10: DOWN
c 53368 Is it too dark for the actor to see? (carry if so)
D 53368 Only for actors that need light: those with +4 bit 7 in their own record (#R$A012($A017)) - the player, the cabs and cabbies, the trains and a few others; everyone else sees in the dark. For them it is dark when inside something, or when the room's record has bit 7 of its first byte clear (outdoors, it seems) and #R$C00C says it is night (8pm to 6am) - unless a light is about: a thing that also has +4 bit 7, whose +5 flags masked with $5C give $14 (full and on, not a person: the oil lamp starts like this, +5 = $94), and that #R$C821 says is here.
D 53368 Confirmed in the emulator (tools/experiments/dark_test.py): walking to Baker Street and setting the clock to 9pm, LOOK gets 'SORRY IT IS TOO DARK.' without the oil lamp and the normal description with it. Moving the player by poking memory instead leaves a carried lamp's own room behind and the test fails - a harness trap worth knowing.
D 53368 How it works:
D 53368 1. Only actors with +4 bit 7 set (the player, and some others) can be in the dark.
D 53368 2. Follow the actor's containment chain (#R$C86E). A room with its light bit (+0 bit 7) is never dark; nor is anywhere by day (anything but night, #R$C00C).
D 53368 3. At night in an unlit room, look through all the things for a light: one that is in the actor's reach, alight (+5 passes the test) and shining (+4 bit 7). If one is found it is not dark.
D 53368 4. Otherwise return with carry set.
R 53368 Input:$A017 The actor's record
R 53368 Output:F Carry when it is too dark for the actor to see; NC when not
