D 55061 The player's turn starts here: the page line count (#R$A012($A019)) is cleared and the text lines go back to full width (#R$C142). Then, unless '@' asked for the last command again (#R$D714), the typed line at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00) is run through the tokenizer (#R$D9C5) word by word into the list at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B81)($5B81): one class byte per token, followed by its two-byte value for words of class $E0, $F0 or $90 and for anything below $80; the list ends at class $C0. #R$D6BD is pointed at the list and the record succeeds. Read from the code.
D 55061 This record is one step of the loop in the player's own plan script at #R$9921: DO LOOK once, then read a line (#R$D6BF), split it into words (#R$D715), check there is a word (#R$D769), parse a sentence (#R$D77E), check there is a sentence (#R$D7D9), turn it into a command (#R$D7FA) and DO it. When you wear the china man's disguise the same script runs for him; the counts below include both.
D 55061 Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over the 15 stored routes): the body ran 15,331 times in 14 of them, run by you, china man. Every one of its 34 instructions was executed (tools/experiments/insn_coverage.py).
D 55061 How it works:
D 55061 1. Clear the page line count and put the text at full width (#R$C142). If '@' was typed, use the last word list again.
D 55061 2. Otherwise clear the 96-byte word list at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B81)($5B81) and read tokens from the typed line (#R$D9C5).
D 55061 3. Store each token's class; classes $E0, $F0, $90 and those below $80 are followed by a two-byte value.
D 55061 4. Continue until the end marker.
