c 55469 The line editor
D 55469 Prints the '> ' prompt and reads up to 128 characters into #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00) through #R$A536: DELETE rubs out (and keeps track of quote marks, #R$D897), $18 clears the line (#R$D9B7), letters, digits and a few punctuation marks are accepted, ENTER ends the line (#R$D949). A time-out from #R$A536 ends it with nothing (#R$D9AC). Read from the code.
D 55469 How it works:
D 55469 1. Print the prompt '> ' and set up the buffer at #LINK(rom_symbols#a5B00)($5B00); B counts down from 128, with bit 7 set while nothing has been typed.
D 55469 2. Wait for a key with the time limit (#R$A536). If time runs out, give up: the player will wait.
D 55469 3. On an empty line, '@' repeats the last command (#R$D99E). The first key of a line may be a cursor key, which is turned into a one-key move (#R$D978).
D 55469 4. Rub-out ($18) clears the whole line; DELETE removes one character, and removing a quote mark flips the inside-quotes flag.
D 55469 5. Letters are taken. A quote mark flips the flag (closing a quotation first ends it with a full stop). Space, full stop, comma, slash, the pound sign, hyphen, colon and digits are taken. Anything else is ignored.
D 55469 6. ENTER closes any open quotation, ends the line with a full stop (#R$D968) and a carriage return, and returns NZ.
R 55469 Output:F NZ if a line was typed, Z if the wait timed out
R 55469 HL The end of the line in the buffer at $5B00
