W 55747,2 The dictionary record being compared
c 55749 Tokenizer: read and classify the next word from input
D 55749 Confirmed, by single-stepping through a real call while typing a real command (see dictionary_notes.md), to be the tokenizer's genuine entry point: skips leading spaces, checks for an empty line or a special character, then (for an ordinary word) calls the per-letter dictionary index lookup (#R$DA34), the word-expansion machinery (#R$A94D), and the comparison routine (#R$DA7D) in turn, repeating for each candidate word stored under the typed word's first letter.
D 55749 Abbreviations, tried in the emulator: EX LAMP, EXA LAMP and EXAM LAMP all examine the oil lamp, EXAMINE LAM too, and INVE takes stock; but EXAMINE LA finds the LABEL and EXAMINE L, TA LAMP and T LAMP give 'WHAT ?', while words with letters added (EXAMINATION, EXAMINEXYZ) are not recognised. So a typed word is matched as the start of a dictionary word (#R$DA7D counts the letters that agree, keeping the best in #R$DC13($DC14); the typed length is #R$DC13), the first word under its letter winning a tie.
D 55749 How it works:
D 55749 1. Skip spaces and remember where the word starts (#R$A022($A029)).
D 55749 2. A carriage return gives an end-of-line token ($C0).
D 55749 3. Characters that are not letters (the pound sign, digits, full stop, comma, quote) are read as their own tokens (#R$DAC6).
D 55749 4. Otherwise look up the dictionary words under the first letter (#R$DA34), spell each one out (#R$A94D) and compare it with the typed word (#R$DA7D), keeping the best match.
D 55749 5. A word that matches returns its class and word reference; no match gives a $D0 token.
R 55749 Input:HL The typed line
R 55749 Output:A, D The token class ($C0 end of line, $D0 not understood, $F0 number, and so on)
R 55749 BC The token's value
R 55749 HL Past the word
R 55749 $A029 Where the word started
