c 55933 Compare the typed word against one dictionary candidate
D 55933 Compares the typed word (already expanded/normalised into a scratch buffer) against one candidate's own expanded spelling, up to the length of whichever is shorter - i.e. genuine abbreviation/prefix matching, not requiring an exact full-length match. On a match, also follows a synonym chain (a bit in the next word's own flag byte marks 'this is the last synonym in the group'; otherwise a 16-bit offset follows, pointing at another word to also accept as equivalent).
D 55933 CONFIRMED WITH REAL DATA, correcting an earlier, wrongly-abandoned conclusion in this project (see dictionary_notes.md): breakpointing this exact address while typing TAKE shows the typed buffer holding 'ake' (the leading 't' already consumed by the per-letter index lookup above) compared in turn against three expanded dictionary candidates - 'able', 'akeS', 'alkS' - almost certainly TABLE, TAKES and TALKS. 'ake' against 'akeS' is exactly a prefix match, real evidence (not just a plausible story) that this comparison, and the word-expansion machinery that produced 'akeS' from a stored stem plus a suffix-table entry, are genuinely how the game recognises typed words.
