c 56579 Default action: move in a direction (verbs 1-10)
D 56579 Shared by all ten direction verbs. The pre-check (#R$DCD2) works out where the actor would end up; the body moves the actor and, when the actor is the one being followed by the display, updates #R$A00A.
D 56579 The body also stores the destination and its room record at #R$DD01 and #R$DCFF - for ANY actor that moves, not just the player. After the boot sequence they hold room 16 (platform one of Aldergate station), left there by someone moving during the first turns. The 'what's here' code uses #R$DCFF, so a harness that teleports the player must set it as well, or the next room description starts with platform one.
D 56579 Seen in the emulator: every compass move by every character, including the cook (EAST and WEST between rooms 65 and 68), the gardener (NORTHWEST, SOUTHWEST, NORTHEAST ...) and the player. In the sampled runs the mover's room (+15) is set to the destination and nothing else changes. #R$DD1D, if it ever ran, would turn a destination of $FF into room 0.
D 56579 Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over the 15 stored routes): the body ran 21,243 times in 14 of them. 20 of its 21 instructions were executed; the rest (#R$DD1D) never ran in any stored route, and were therefore read from the code only.
D 56579 How it works:
D 56579 1. The pre-check (#R$DCD2) finds the exit in the direction (the verb number), and refuses if there is none, the actor is inside something, the destination is 0 or the thing in the way will not let the actor through.
D 56579 2. The body sets the displayed room if the actor is the one followed, remembers the room entered (#R$DD01) and its record (#R$DCFF).
D 56579 3. The actor's own location (+15) becomes the new room, and the things it holds are updated (#R$D249).
R 56579 Input:IX The exit found by the pre-check
R 56579 Output:F NZ; the actor is in the new room
