c 56784 Default action: WAIT UNTIL (verb 50)
D 56784 Works out how many minutes there are from now until the time typed (the number at #R$A02A($A02F): hour and minute in 6-bit fields, bit 6 of the hour marking am and bit 6 of the minute pm), converting both times to minutes past 12 o'clock (#R$DE48: hours x 60 + minutes, 12 counting as 0) and, when neither am nor pm was typed, taking whichever comes next; the result becomes the time the action takes (#R$A01A($A01F)), so the player's next turn simply comes that much later (see the main loop at #R$A064, which also lets ENTER cut the wait short). #R$DDCB-#R$DDCF hold the two times and the am/pm letter. Read from the code.
D 56784 Tried in the emulator 2026-11-08, at 8:01am on Monday: WAIT UNTIL 9:00 sets the action time to 59 minutes ($89 $00 at #R$A02A($A02F): bit 7 marks a time); WAIT UNTIL 8 to 719 (8pm, the next 8 o'clock); WAIT UNTIL 8:30PM ($88 $5E, bit 6 of the minutes) to 749; WAIT UNTIL 7AM ($C7 $00, bit 6 of the hour) to 1379, 7am the next day. WAIT UNTIL 8:30 PM, with a space, gets I DO NOT KNOW THE WORD "PM". All four waits are cut short at 8:34 by the news about Lestrade.
D 56784 CORRECTED: the automatic split had most of this body as text and data.
D 56784 How it works:
D 56784 1. Take the time typed (#R$A02A($A02F)) and work out the minutes past twelve for it (#R$DE48) and for the clock now.
D 56784 2. If am or pm was typed, the target is in that half of the day; otherwise it is in the same half as now, or the next.
D 56784 3. The difference is how long the action takes (#R$A01A($A01F)).
R 56784 Input:$A02F The time typed
R 56784 Output:$A01F The minutes to wait
R 56784 F NZ
