c 57049 Pre-check for PUT IN: will the thing fit in the container?
D 57049 Sets IX to the thing being moved (#R$A012($A013)), IY to the container (#R$A012($A015)) and A to the container's id, then falls into the fit check at #R$DEE4 - which the SIT IN / GET INTO pre-check (#R$E3F5) also jumps into with different registers (IX = the actor, IY = the chair): a shared tail.
D 57049 #R$DEE4 refuses if none of bits 0-2 of the container's field +4 are set (it cannot hold things). Otherwise it fits if capacity - size - what's already inside is at least 1, where capacity and size are both field +2 and #R$D2ED totals what is already inside.
D 57049 Confirmed in the emulator, including the boundary: Holmes (size 96) gets into Watson's armchair (capacity 255) even with Watson in it (#R$D2ED returns 96): 255-96-96 = 63. Poking the armchair's capacity to 193 still lets him in (1 to spare); 192 refuses (0 to spare), and the refusal is reported as 'I SEE NO WATSONS ARMCHAIR THAT YOU CAN GET INTO'.
R 57049 Input:$A013 The thing being put
R 57049 $A015 The container
R 57049 $A035 The container's id
R 57049 Output:IX The thing
R 57049 IY The container
R 57049 F NZ if it fits; Z if it will not
C 57049 IX = the thing being put somewhere
C 57053 IY = the container
C 57057 A = the container's id
C 57060 B = the container's id. Can the container hold things?
C 57061 Read the type byte (+4) of the thing record at IY into A
C 57064 Keep only bits 0, 1, 2 of A (mask $07)
C 57066 No: refuse
C 57069 Capacity minus the size of the thing...
C 57072 Subtract the size byte (+2) of the thing record at IX from A
C 57075 ...too big even for an empty container? Refuse
C 57077 Save AF on the stack
C 57078 How much is already inside the container?
C 57079 Call the routine "Total size (+2) of the things directly in thing A" (#R$D2ED)
C 57082 Copy A into B
C 57083 Restore AF from the stack
C 57084 Take that off too
C 57085 Not enough room: refuse
C 57087 At least 1 to spare: it fits (NZ)
C 57088 Exactly full also refuses (Z)
C 57089 Return to the caller
