b 57214 Sub-script: shared action 20, TAKE the burning note
D 57214 TAKE it (#R$DCC1), then #R$DF8B. Read from the script.
D 57214 Entry points: #R$DF80 = body of record #R$DF7E.
D 57214 Decoded by tools/plan.py; see #R$CC78 for the language. Each sub-block is one instruction. Room and object numbers are as the game uses them (the reference pages list both). Labels for verbs 128-140 and for the common helper records (#R$E52F, #R$E57A, #R$E588, #R$E5A8, #R$DDBE, #R$F198) were read from those records' code, not tried one by one in the emulator.
D 57214 In an instruction, 'plan dobj' and 'plan iobj' are not fixed objects: they mean the direct and indirect object currently held in the actor's plan registers (bytes +4 and +5 of its actor record), so the same step works on whatever the actor is dealing with - for the player, the objects of the command just typed. 'plan verb' is the verb register (+3), 'self' is the acting character, and '-' means none.
D 57214 Played: DROP BURN NOTE then a plain TAKE BURN NOTE runs this script - the countdown #R$9CA1 goes to $FF and the note is called SINGE; EXAMINE CLOSELY SINGE NOTE then counts as examining it. TAKE NOTE FROM FIREPLACE takes the note through a different action and leaves the countdown running. Note that typing BURNING NOTE is read as BURNT (the dictionary shares BURN), so the note must be called BURN NOTE until it is singed.
B 57214,2 Action record header: flags $88; bit 7: checks only; byte 1 $00 - checks-only: the script follows
B 57216,5 RUN #R$DCC1 TAKE (dobj) (plan dobj, -)
B 57221,5 RUN #R$DF8B (plan dobj, -)
B 57226,1 RETURN OK
c 57227 Action record: singe the note when it is picked up (RUN at $DF85)
D 57227 Addresses named in the title: #R$DF85.
