D 57653 Addresses named in the title: #R$E0A7, #R$E0E2, #R$E0F4.
D 57653 EXAMINE CLOSELY. The body, for the player only (actor 0), prints the description (#R$DF5B) and then looks the thing up in #R$E16B, a table of three-byte entries (thing, message address) ended by $FF: the bed (76), the window (102), the bridge (57), the drawer (62) and the corpse (46) each have a longer text of their own. Read from the code.
D 57653 Confirmed in the emulator 2026-11-08, each in its own room: EXAMINE CLOSELY BED (room 61) -> 'The bed is unmade and one sheet is crumpled up on top. In one corner of the sheet is a very small tear.'; CORPSE (room 88) -> dried blood on the face and 'dark speckled markings that resemble powder burns' on the right hand; BRIDGE (room 88) -> 'a small chip in the stone work that is evidently quite new. It looks like a heavy metallic object has struck it.'; DRAWER (room 72, the Browns' study) -> 'the drawer has a false bottom.' These are clues the plain EXAMINE does not give.
D 57653 Seen: the thing is marked as seen (+6 bit 6) when examined closely - the sandstone bridge, the crumpled note, the body, the bloodstained clothes. The table at #R$E16B is data (thing, message pairs) that the disassembler listed as code, which is why the last 12 'instructions' were not executed.
D 57653 Seen in the emulator (9 November, tools/experiments/record_coverage.py over the 15 stored routes): the body ran 177 times in 11 of them, run by you. 19 of its 31 instructions were executed; the rest (#R$E16B #R$E16C #R$E16F #R$E170 #R$E171 #R$E172 #R$E173 #R$E174 #R$E176 #R$E177 ...) never ran in any stored route, and were therefore read from the code only.
