D 58055 Addresses named in the title: #R$AA71.
D 58055 CORRECTED - this entry previously called this 'a shared generic message/fallback handler reached by INVENTORY, WAIT and STAND UP'. Logging the actor and verb at each hit showed every one of those hits was actor 2 doing HAIL (verb #R$E2C7(31)) on object 30, which simply happened to run on the same turns as the player's commands. The player's INVENTORY/WAIT/STAND UP never reach it. It is reached through object action lists that map HAIL (and, for the same object, GET) to shared action 11 - see #R$8F06.
D 58055 Also CORRECTED: this entry previously described a 'self-modifying-code trick' storing into #R$E2C6. #R$E2C6 follows a RET at #R$E2C5 and is never executed - it is an ordinary one-byte variable kept just in front of the record, not code.
D 58055 Record header: flags $47 (bit 6: pre-check routine at #R$E298), byte 1 $10; body at #R$E2CB. The body remembers the player's room (#R$A00A) and the hailed cab's id + 1 (the cabbie) in #R$E2C6, calls #R$DD0E to move the cab into the player's room, then calls #R$E2EE to start the cabbie on the script at #R$F2B6 with the hailer and the cab as objects. Confirmed in the emulator (tools/experiments/cab_test.py): HAIL CAB in Baker Street prints 'You hail the hansom cab.', the cabbie says 'Hello guv'nor.' and the next LOOK shows the cab with the cabbie in the street.
R 58055 Input:$A034 The cab hailed
R 58055 Output:F NZ; the cab moved to the player and the cabbie started on its fare script
