b 60971 Plan script: Basil Phipps (actor 6) - the spy with the plans
D 60971 Entry point: #R$EE2B = phipps (actor 6).
D 60971 Decoded by tools/plan.py; see #R$CC78 for the language. Each sub-block is one instruction. Split off from the block before, which it merely follows in memory.
D 60971 In an instruction, 'plan dobj' and 'plan iobj' are not fixed objects: they mean the direct and indirect object currently held in the actor's plan registers (bytes +4 and +5 of its actor record), so the same step works on whatever the actor is dealing with - for the player, the objects of the command just typed. 'plan verb' is the verb register (+3), 'self' is the acting character, and '-' means none.
D 60971 His week, logged in the emulator with nobody interfering (tools/experiments/phipps_route.py, 3pm and Major endings poked out of the way): Monday off stage from 3pm, by train and cab to Camden Street and Basil's house on Tuesday morning (rooms 32, 33), back out on Wednesday at 9:55am, by cab and train to Leatherhead and on to Old Mill Road (room 103) at 12:10pm.
D 60971 At Old Mill Road (#R$EF26) he waits for 3pm, then starts a countdown of 16 minutes plus a random number (#R$EF34). Each minute he checks whether you are at Old Mill Road (#R$EF39). If the countdown runs out first he leaves the game for good (#R$EF8C) - logged at 3:20pm - and the plans are sold: at 10am on Thursday Lestrade's script ends the game with the papers. If you arrive in time, 'AS YOU ENTER HE WHIPS HIS HORSES INTO ACTION AND SPEEDS OFF DOWN THE ROAD.' (#R$EF47, message #R$7573): he is put in Leatherhead's Main Street, waits for 4pm and 48 minutes more, walks to the station, takes a train to platform four in London, walks five steps south-west to Kings Cross Road, takes a cab to Buckingham Palace Road and walks east twice, to platform one at Victoria (room 30). If you are there (#R$EF83) he runs #R$9E48 - the last scene; if not, he leaves the game (#R$EF8C).
D 60971 The last scene was staged in the emulator (tools/experiments/endgame_test.py): you on platform one at Victoria, Phipps's script pointed at #R$EF7F in Buckingham Palace Road. With Watson and Lestrade beside you: 'Basil Phipps enters. ... As Basil enters he sees you and pulls a gun. With suprising agility Watson pushes you out of the way of Basils first shot allowing Lestrade time to pull his gun and capture the culprits. As incredible as it may seem you have solved this case. You truly deserve the title of worlds greatest detective.' Without Lestrade, or without Watson: '...With his usual skill his first shot disposes of you. Perhaps you should try again.' With you one room away, he walked in and left the game.
D 60971 The flight from #R$EF4A on is shared with the German agent (#R$EFF6), who waits at Old Mill Road too.
D 60971 Played, Wednesday morning with the player outside in Camden Street or in Basil's front yard: Phipps is in the bedroom (room 35) through the night, in the hall (33) at 10:06, in Camden Street (32) at 10:09 and in his cab (room 1) at 10:15 - the same whether the player is in room 102 or in the street. The 9:55am in the earlier phipps_route.py log came from a run with five-minute WAITs and nothing done in the case, and was not reproduced. At 9:50am he shoots whoever is in the bedroom, in disguise or not.
D 60971 Why Phipps's Wednesday morning differs between logs (9 November): at #R$EEE9 his script tests whether you are in Camden Street or Basil's front yard, or the china man is in either. If so he plays the messenger scene (#R$EFAC) and waits 15 minutes before leaving the bedroom; if not he goes at once. With the player indoors he is in Camden Street at 9:55am; with the player in the front yard, as in the winning route, about 10:06-10:10.
