c 62402 Draw a picture (the picture interpreter)
D 62402 HL = the picture's bytecode (from the table at #R$F60C via #R$A277). The pen starts at (127, 63). Commands, read from the code: $00 end; $08 move the pen (next two bytes); bit 7 set: draw a line (#R$F56E); bit 6: fill (#R$F490); bit 5: colour runs of attribute cells. Plots through the ROM's PIXEL-ADD (#LINK(rom_symbols#a22AA)($22AA)), which is why the harness skips it.
D 62402 Confirmed in the emulator: with SkoolKit's copy of the 48K ROM at $0000, calling this for each of the 13 pictures draws a recognisable scene (Baker Street's lamp post and door, the entrance hallway's stairs, a grand piano, Brown's house at night...) in the top right quarter of the screen and returns. The game harness still skips it, to keep play fast.
D 62402 How it works:
D 62402 1. The pen starts at (127, 63). Take the next command; 0 ends the picture.
D 62402 2. $08 moves the pen to the (x, y) in the next two bytes (x always has bit 7 set, so the pen stays in the right half of the screen).
D 62402 3. A command with bit 7 set draws a line (#R$F56E): its low three bits give the direction, and the rest the number of steps and run lengths.
D 62402 4. Other commands set the colour, fill an area (#R$F490) or draw other shapes; each is followed by its operands.
R 62402 Input:HL The picture's bytecode
R 62402 Output:The picture drawn; registers kept
