C 62764 Compare A with $5A
C 62766 If A differs from $5A, skip ahead to #R$F531($F532)
C 62768 Clear the carry flag, so that the subtraction that follows is a plain one (a compare)
C 62769 Subtract DE from HL (a compare: carry = HL was smaller, zero = equal)
C 62771 Restore DE from the stack
C 62772 Restore AF from the stack
C 62773 Return to the caller
C 62774 Save AF on the stack - HL one byte back, unless that leaves the attribute area
C 62775 Step HL back one byte
C 62776 Copy H into A
C 62777 Compare A with $57
C 62779 If A differs from $57, skip ahead to #R$F53E
C 62781 Step HL on to the next byte
C 62782 Restore AF from the stack
C 62783 Return to the caller
C 62784 Save AF on the stack - HL one byte on, unless that leaves the attribute area
C 62785 Step HL on to the next byte
C 62786 Copy H into A
C 62787 Compare A with $5A
C 62789 If A differs from $5A, skip ahead to #R$F548
C 62791 Step HL back one byte
C 62792 Restore AF from the stack
C 62793 Return to the caller
c 62794 Move the pen one pixel (Z when it would leave the picture area)
D 62794 E is the pen's y and D its x. #R$F54A moves down (stopping at 176), #R$F555 up (stopping at 0), #R$F55E right (stopping at 255), #R$F565 left - stopping when bit 7 of D would clear, so the pen can never leave the right half of the screen, which is why every picture sits there.
R 62794 Input:E The pen's y
R 62794 Output:E One more unless it would reach 176
R 62794 F Z at the edge
C 62794 Add one to E - One pixel up (E + 1) - unless that reaches y = 176 ($B0): then put it back and Z
C 62795 Copy A into H
C 62796 Copy E into A
C 62797 Compare A with $B0
C 62799 Copy H into A
C 62800 If A differs from $B0, return
C 62801 Subtract one from E
C 62802 Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
C 62803 Copy H into A
C 62804 Return to the caller
C 62805 Subtract one from E - One down (E - 1), not below 0
C 62806 Copy A into H
C 62807 Copy E into A
C 62808 Compare A with $FF
C 62810 Copy H into A
C 62811 If A differs from $FF, return
C 62812 Add one to E
C 62813 Return to the caller
C 62814 Add one to D - One right (D + 1), not past 255
C 62815 If D is not zero, return
C 62816 Subtract one from D
C 62817 Copy A into H
C 62818 Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
C 62819 Copy H into A
C 62820 Return to the caller
C 62821 Subtract one from D - One left (D - 1), not out of the right half (bit 7 of D must stay set)
C 62822 Test bit 7 of D
C 62824 If bit 7 of D is set, return
C 62825 Add one to D
C 62826 Copy A into H
C 62827 Clear A to zero (and the carry flag)
C 62828 Copy H into A
C 62829 Return to the caller
c 62830 Draw a line from the pen
D 62830 L = the number of steps, B the run length before a step on the minor axis, C the directions (bit 0: which axis is major; bits 1 and 2: up or down, left or right). Plots each point with #R$F5D2. Read from the code.
R 62830 Input:L The number of steps
R 62830 B The run length before a step on the other axis
R 62830 C The direction bits
R 62830 D, E The pen
R 62830 Output:D, E The pen after the line
R 62830 BC, HL Kept
C 62830 Test bit 0 of C - Bit 0 set: the line is mostly along E
C 62832 If bit 0 of C is set, jump on to #R$F5A2
C 62834 Save HL on the stack
C 62835 Save BC on the stack
C 62836 Mostly along D: plot the point
C 62839 Test bit 2 of C - Step along D: left with bit 2, right without
C 62841 If bit 2 of C is clear, skip ahead to #R$F582
C 62843 Call #R$F565 (inside "Move the pen one pixel (Z when it would...")
C 62846 If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$F59F
C 62848 Skip ahead to #R$F587
C 62850 Call #R$F55E (inside "Move the pen one pixel (Z when it would...")
C 62853 If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$F59F
C 62855 Subtract one from B - End of a run?
C 62856 If B is not zero, skip ahead to #R$F59C
C 62858 Test bit 1 of C - Then one step along E: E - 1 with bit 1, E + 1 without
C 62860 If bit 1 of C is clear, skip ahead to #R$F595
C 62862 Call #R$F555 (inside "Move the pen one pixel (Z when it would...")
C 62865 If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$F59F
C 62867 Skip ahead to #R$F59A
C 62869 Call the routine "Move the pen one pixel (Z when it would leave the..." (#R$F54A)
C 62872 If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$F59F
C 62874 Restore BC from the stack - and the run length again
C 62875 Save BC on the stack
C 62876 Subtract one from L - L points in all
C 62877 If L is not zero, go back to #R$F574
C 62879 Restore BC from the stack - (or until the pen meets the edge of the picture)
C 62880 Restore HL from the stack
C 62881 Return to the caller
C 62882 Save HL on the stack
C 62883 Save BC on the stack
C 62884 Mostly along E: plot the point
C 62887 Test bit 1 of C - Step along E: E - 1 with bit 1, E + 1 without
C 62889 If bit 1 of C is clear, skip ahead to #R$F5B2
C 62891 Call #R$F555 (inside "Move the pen one pixel (Z when it would...")
C 62894 If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$F5CF
C 62896 Skip ahead to #R$F5B7
C 62898 Call the routine "Move the pen one pixel (Z when it would leave the..." (#R$F54A)
C 62901 If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$F5CF
C 62903 Subtract one from B - End of a run?
C 62904 If B is not zero, skip ahead to #R$F5CC
C 62906 Test bit 2 of C - Then one step along D: left with bit 2, right without
C 62908 If bit 2 of C is clear, skip ahead to #R$F5C5
C 62910 Call #R$F565 (inside "Move the pen one pixel (Z when it would...")
C 62913 If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$F5CF
C 62915 Skip ahead to #R$F5CA
C 62917 Call #R$F55E (inside "Move the pen one pixel (Z when it would...")
C 62920 If the zero flag is set, skip ahead to #R$F5CF
C 62922 Restore BC from the stack - and the run length again
C 62923 Save BC on the stack
C 62924 Subtract one from L - L points in all
C 62925 If L is not zero, go back to #R$F5A4
C 62927 Restore BC from the stack
C 62928 Restore HL from the stack
C 62929 Return to the caller
c 62930 Plot the pen position in the current colour
D 62930 Sets the pixel at (D, E) and gives its character cell the ink in #R$F60B, keeping the cell's paper.
D 62930 How it works:
D 62930 1. Find the screen byte and bit for the pixel at (D, E) (#R$F5FB).
D 62930 2. Work out the attribute byte for that character cell.
D 62930 3. Keep the cell's paper and set its ink to the pen colour at #R$F60B. If the paper is already that colour, use the opposite ink so the pixel shows.
D 62930 4. Set the pixel itself.
R 62930 Input:D X coordinate
R 62930 E Y coordinate
R 62930 HL is preserved; A and F are not
C 62930 Save HL on the stack
C 62931 Call the routine "Find the screen byte and bit for the pen" (#R$F5FB) - HL = the pixel's screen byte, A its bit (#R$F5FB)
C 62934 Save AF on the stack
C 62935 Save HL on the stack
C 62936 Copy H into A - Find its attribute byte ($58xx)
C 62937 Keep only bits 3, 4 of A (mask $18)
C 62939 Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
C 62940 Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
C 62941 Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
C 62942 Add $58 to A
C 62944 Copy A into H
C 62945 Read the byte HL points at into A - Keep the paper, set the ink to the pen colour (#R$F60B)
C 62946 Keep only bits 3, 4, 5 of A (mask $38)
C 62948 Store A at the address HL points to
C 62949 Read "Fill pattern for the picture interpreter" (#R$F60B) into A
C 62952 Rotate A left one place (bit 7 goes to carry and bit 0)
C 62953 Rotate A left one place (bit 7 goes to carry and bit 0)
C 62954 Rotate A left one place (bit 7 goes to carry and bit 0)
C 62955 Compare A with the byte HL points at - (if the paper is that colour too, use the opposite ink)
C 62956 If A differs from the byte HL points at, skip ahead to #R$F5F0
C 62958 Flip in A the bits that are set in $38
C 62960 Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
C 62961 Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
C 62962 Rotate A right one place (bit 0 goes to carry and bit 7)
C 62963 Set in A the bits that are set in the byte HL points at
C 62964 Store A at the address HL points to
C 62965 Restore HL from the stack - Then set the pixel
C 62966 Restore AF from the stack
C 62967 Set in A the bits that are set in the byte HL points at
C 62968 Store A at the address HL points to
C 62969 Restore HL from the stack
C 62970 Return to the caller
c 62971 Find the screen byte and bit for the pen
D 62971 The ROM's PIXEL-ADD (#LINK(rom_symbols#a22AA)($22AA)) with B = y and C = x, then a mask for the bit.
R 62971 Input:D, E The pen position
R 62971 Output:HL The screen byte
R 62971 A The bit mask
R 62971 BC Kept
C 62971 Save BC on the stack - B = E (y), C = D (x): the ROM's pixel address routine (#LINK(rom_symbols#a22AA)($22AA)) gives HL = the screen byte, A = the bit position
C 62972 Copy E into B
C 62973 Copy D into C
C 62974 Call the ROM pixel-address routine (#LINK(rom_symbols#a22AA)($22AA))
C 62977 Copy A into B - Turn the position into a mask: one bit rotated into place
C 62978 Add one to B
C 62979 Load A with $01
C 62981 Rotate A right
C 62983 Count B down and, while it is not zero, go back to #R$F605
C 62985 Restore BC from the stack - Return the screen byte in HL and the mask in A
C 62986 Return to the caller
b 62987 Fill pattern for the picture interpreter
D 62987 Set by #R$F490 (A on entry) and used while filling. CORRECTED: the automatic split had this as code.
B 62987,1 Fill pattern
b 62988 Which rooms have a picture
D 62988 14 three-byte entries (room, address of its picture), ended by $FF; #R$A0D8 points here and #R$A25D searches it (#R$D34E) when a room is described with pictures switched on (#R$A00A($A011)). Rooms 54 and 55 share one picture.
B 62988,3 Room 5 (baker street): #R$FF47
B 62991,3 Room 74 (daphnes room): #R$FE82
B 62994,3 Room 71 (browns library): #R$FDA8
B 62997,3 Room 89 (west side): #R$FCED
B 63000,3 Room 88 (east side): #R$FC3A
B 63003,3 Room 54 (platform one): #R$FB6E
B 63006,3 Room 55 (platform two): #R$FB6E
B 63009,3 Room 11 (scotland yard): #R$FAAA
B 63012,3 Room 62 (browns front gate): #R$F9F0
B 63015,3 Room 6 (entrance hallway): #R$F928
B 63018,3 Room 92 (jones front gate): #R$F868
B 63021,3 Room 95 (small room): #R$F7BE
B 63024,3 Room 61 (basils bedroom): #R$F6ED
B 63027,3 Room 12 (slater street): #R$F638
B 63030,2 End marker, and a spare byte
b 63032 Picture: room 12 (slater street)
D 63032 Picture bytecode for #R$F3C2, read from the interpreter: $00 ends it; $08 x y moves the pen; a byte with bit 7 set draws a line (direction and length packed into it and the next byte, #R$F56E); bit 6 fills from a point with a pattern (#R$F490); bit 5 colours runs of character cells (an attribute address, then length/direction bytes up to $FF). Drawn in the emulator with the ROM loaded (tools/draw_pictures.py): see the Pictures page.
D 63032 CORRECTED: the automatic split had parts of this as code and text, and an earlier version of this overlay called #R$FBE6($FBED) and #R$FDE8($FDED) 'data, not decoded'.
